  \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammonia-containing solutions or moist films, approximately pH 8-9.5; cyanide trapping must compete with hydrolysis and formaldehyde-ammonia oligomer formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to formaldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible water elimination to methanimine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the imine-forming component of classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850, reaction-class precedent). A high equilibrium concentration of free methanimine is not required if its downstream capture is sufficiently rapid. Aqueous hydrolysis and formation of other formaldehyde-ammonia condensates limit selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of methanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Buffered HCN/CN- solution, approximately pH 7.5-9; methanimine is generated locally rather than isolated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to give aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the defining carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the Strecker reaction class (Strecker, 1850). Formaldehyde supplies the eventual glycine methylene carbon, and the added cyanide supplies the carboxyl carbon. Direct interception of photogenerated methanimine remains a kinetic competition, not an established high-yield geological process.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7.5-9.5; HCN/CN- available; cyanohydrin formation remains reversible.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the formaldehyde carbonyl, and protonation of the resulting alkoxide yields glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation is established chemistry and is integral to HCN-based prebiotic homologation networks (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). Glycolonitrile can act as a chemically real carbonyl-cyanide reservoir. It is not assumed to be indefinitely stable against dissociation, hydrolysis or further photochemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-mediated conversion of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammonia-containing solution, approximately pH 8-10; cyanohydrin, carbonyl, imine and cyanide species interconvert.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion occurs through cyanohydrin dissociation, carbonyl-imine exchange and cyanide recapture. Uncatalyzed displacement of hydroxide from an alcohol is not assumed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This represents the cyanohydrin-entry branch of the Strecker reaction manifold, not a separate elementary substitution mechanism. It is chemically justified when glycolonitrile accumulates before ammonia exposure. The distinct pathway is therefore an alternative reservoir and mixing history, not evidence for a wholly independent glycine-synthesis family.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions for acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"380-430 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 20-200 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Locally acidic fluid, approximately pH 2-4; prolonged residence may be necessary; organic material must avoid the hottest vent fluid.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation facilitates water addition; proton transfers and tautomerization generate the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile-to-amide hydration is an established first stage of Strecker-product hydrolysis. The exact rate at natural acidity and concentration is not established here; many laboratory hydrolyses use stronger acid. Transport and dilution of surface-produced aminoacetonitrile are additional unresolved bottlenecks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions for acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-440 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 20-200 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 2-4; ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions; cool products after sufficient hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl, followed by tetrahedral-intermediate collapse and release of ammonia or ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis completes the classical conversion of an aminonitrile skeleton into an amino acid. It can be slower than nitrile hydration and is a principal kinetic bottleneck. Natural residence times, actual acid activity and competing glycine degradation must be assessed before assigning a yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-dioxide capture and hydantoin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammonium/inorganic-carbon solution, approximately pH 7-9; dissolved CO2 is replenished by its bicarbonate equilibrium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminoacetonitrile captures CO2 to form a carbamate-derived intermediate, followed by cyclization and rearrangement through the Bucherer-Bergs reaction manifold to hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin formation from carbonyl compounds, cyanide and ammonium carbonate is established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934). The displayed aminonitrile-plus-CO2 reaction is a net representation of its downstream cyclization chemistry. Hydantoin can sequester the glycine skeleton, but its formation requires suitable concentrations and its later hydrolysis is harder than formation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions for acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"410-450 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 30-200 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Locally acidic aqueous compartment, approximately pH 2-3; extended heating; avoid drying that favors ring closure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic opening of the cyclic imide produces N-carbamoylglycine, represented by its neutral hydantoic-acid formula.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoins are established hydrolysable amino-acid precursors in Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. Applying acidic hydrolysis at these geological conditions is a conditional extrapolation: practical laboratory procedures can use stronger acid or alkali. Slow ring opening and reverse cyclization are important limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoic-acid hydrolysis and decarbamoylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions for acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"420-450 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 30-200 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3 in a locally acidic fluid; prolonged heating may be needed; neutral formulas summarize actual protonated products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis removes the carbamoyl group. The resulting carbamic-acid-derived fragment decomposes to ammonia and CO2, leaving glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the chemically established hydantoin-to-amino-acid transformation while explicitly retaining the ring-opened intermediate. The proposed natural implementation is uncertain because urea-bond cleavage can require harsh conditions. The CO2 incorporated during hydantoin formation is released again and does not become either carbon atom of glycine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Accessible metallic nickel sites in native Ni-Fe alloy, such as awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 50-200 bar with substantial hydrogen partial pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy with active metallic nickel sites\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-9; ammonia-rich anoxic water; surface passivation and sulfide poisoning must be limited.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylic acid reversibly condenses with ammonia to form an imine-derived species. Surface-activated hydrogen reduces its carbon-nitrogen double bond, giving glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive amination is established chemistry, but the performance of a natural awaruite surface for this exact substrate and condition set is a speculative geochemical extrapolation here. Nickel is proposed specifically for hydrogen activation, not merely because a mineral is present. Competing carbonyl reduction to glycolate, glyoxylate hydration and catalyst poisoning could prevent useful selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron-driven reductive amination of glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native Fe(0), a consumed reducing mineral also represented explicitly as mol_016\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-410 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 20-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 6-8 with buffering and sufficient total ammonia; Fe(0) oxidizes to Fe(II). The inferred net stoichiometry consumes two proton equivalents per glycine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia and glyoxylic acid establish an imine-derived intermediate. Iron corrosion supplies reducing equivalents, directly or through locally generated hydrogen, that can reduce this intermediate to glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a speculative mineral-specific implementation of reductive amination, not a claim of demonstrated selectivity under the stated conditions. Fe(0) is a stoichiometric reductant rather than a catalyst, and its oxidation product is included for redox accounting. Native-iron availability, passivation, competing hydrogen evolution and preferential glyoxylate-to-glycolate reduction are critical uncertainties. It differs from rxn_013 in its electron source and dependence on mineral consumption.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Methanol-derived imine-entry Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Photochemically generated formaldehyde enters the imine branch, is trapped by cyanide, and undergoes sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis. The methanol photochemical entry is conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Methanol-derived cyanohydrin-entry Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Methanol-derived formaldehyde is first stored as glycolonitrile. Subsequent ammonia exposure accesses aminoacetonitrile, followed by hydrolysis to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct capture of HCN-derived methanimine\",\n      \"description\": \"A first HCN carbon is photoreduced to methanimine and intercepted by additional cyanide before hydrolysis. The resulting aminoacetonitrile is hydrolysed to glycine; successful imine interception is conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN photoreduction through a glycolonitrile reservoir\",\n      \"description\": \"Photogenerated methanimine hydrolyses to formaldehyde, which captures additional cyanide. Ammonia-mediated conversion of the glycolonitrile reservoir gives the aminonitrile that is hydrolysed to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Methanol-derived imine entry with hydantoin sequestration\",\n      \"description\": \"The methanol-to-imine entry supplies aminoacetonitrile, which captures inorganic carbon as hydantoin rather than undergoing immediate nitrile hydration. Ring opening and decarbamoylation release glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Methanol-derived cyanohydrin entry with hydantoin sequestration\",\n      \"description\": \"A glycolonitrile reservoir formed from methanol-derived formaldehyde is converted to aminoacetonitrile. Hydantoin formation then precedes hydrolytic release of glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct HCN-derived imine capture followed by hydantoin formation\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanide interception of photogenerated methanimine produces aminoacetonitrile. Inorganic-carbon capture stores the skeleton as hydantoin, whose subsequent hydrolysis gives glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-derived cyanohydrin reservoir followed by hydantoin formation\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN photoreduction supplies formaldehyde through methanimine hydrolysis. Sequential glycolonitrile and aminoacetonitrile formation leads to hydantoin sequestration and eventual glycine release.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-fed mineral-catalysed glyoxylate reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylic acid and ammonia are reductively coupled using hydrogen on a proposed native Ni-Fe catalytic surface. This bypasses cyanide and hydantoin chemistry; the mineral-specific implementation remains speculative.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Sacrificial-native-iron glyoxylate reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylic acid and ammonia are reductively coupled using electrons from consumed native iron. Fe(II) formation distinguishes this conditional route from hydrogen-fed catalytic reduction.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_020\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "Construct ten distinct feedstock-and-branch sequences: eight traverse the experimentally grounded Strecker/cyanohydrin reaction manifold, with either direct nitrile hydrolysis or hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis; two use reductive amination of glyoxylic acid with different electron sources. These are ten network pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic mechanisms. Methanol photodehydrogenation and mineral-specific reductive amination are conditional, partly speculative branches, identified in their reaction reasoning. Methanol, HCN, ammonia, sulfide, inorganic carbon, hydrogen and glyoxylic acid are supplied feedstocks; their environmental production is outside this network. Surface-generated aminonitriles or hydantoins require transfer into heated aqueous compartments for the hydrolysis conditions proposed here, and successful transport is not established. Twenty molecule nodes and fourteen reaction nodes retain the principal hydrolysis intermediates explicitly. All reactions exclude molecular oxygen. Organic formulas use neutral reference species; actual aqueous protonation and hydration depend on pH. The schema has no coproduct role, so terminal sulfur and Fe(II) coproducts are labeled intermediate for bookkeeping only. No end-to-end yield or naturally operating integrated network is claimed.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH4O", "iupac_name": "Methanol", "common_name": "Methanol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4O/c1-2/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "HCN", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Ammonia", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "CH3N", "iupac_name": "Methanimine", "common_name": "Formaldimine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "Glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-Aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "Aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-Aminoacetamide", "common_name": "Glycinamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H4N2O2", "iupac_name": "Imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H6N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(Carbamoylamino)acetic acid", "common_name": "Hydantoic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-Oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "Iron", "common_name": "Native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "Hydron", "common_name": "Proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "Iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "Cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-Aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Anoxic partial photodehydrogenation of methanol", "inputs": ["mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Anatase, TiO2, as a semiconductor photocatalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Anatase", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic methanol-containing water; UV wavelengths capable of exciting TiO2; formaldehyde must be removed or trapped before further oxidation."}, "mechanism": "Photogenerated holes oxidize adsorbed methanol to formaldehyde, while conduction-band electrons reduce protons to hydrogen. The displayed reaction is the partial-dehydrogenation endpoint, not complete conversion to CO2.", "reasoning": "Conditional, partly speculative prebiotic branch. Methanol photodehydrogenation is chemically established, but useful hydrogen evolution commonly depends on deliberately prepared catalysts or cocatalysts. Natural anatase is justified by its semiconductor chemistry, not assumed to provide preparative yields. Methanol concentration, charge recombination and further formaldehyde oxidation are major bottlenecks; no oxygen is required."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreduction of HCN to transient methanimine", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved copper-cyanide photoredox complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic UV-irradiated water, approximately pH 7-9; sulfide supplies reducing equivalents; cyanide complexation and copper-sulfide precipitation compete."}, "mechanism": "Successive electron-transfer and protonation events partially reduce HCN to methanimine. Sulfide is oxidized; S8 represents an elemental-sulfur endpoint rather than a claim that sulfur radicals and polysulfides are absent.", "reasoning": "Cyanosulfidic photoredox chemistry supports reduction of HCN-derived carbon toward aldehydes (Patel et al., 2015). Methanimine is a mechanistic intermediate rather than an assumed isolable product. Its interception by additional cyanide is conditional because hydrolysis and further reduction compete. Sulfur speciation and copper availability make the displayed net step a simplification of the photochemical system."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Methanimine hydrolysis to formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6-9; water-rich conditions; formaldehyde is substantially hydrated in solution."}, "mechanism": "Water adds to the imine carbon, producing a transient carbinolamine that undergoes carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage.", "reasoning": "Imine hydrolysis provides the aldehyde-forming exit from HCN photoreduction, consistent with the cyanosulfidic framework of Patel et al. (2015). Rapid hydrolysis prevents treating methanimine as a durable transported feedstock. This reaction and rxn_004 describe opposite directions of the same chemical equilibrium, not independent reaction families."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonia-containing solutions or moist films, approximately pH 8-9.5; cyanide trapping must compete with hydrolysis and formaldehyde-ammonia oligomer formation."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to formaldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible water elimination to methanimine.", "reasoning": "This is the imine-forming component of classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850, reaction-class precedent). A high equilibrium concentration of free methanimine is not required if its downstream capture is sufficiently rapid. Aqueous hydrolysis and formation of other formaldehyde-ammonia condensates limit selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanide capture of methanimine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Buffered HCN/CN- solution, approximately pH 7.5-9; methanimine is generated locally rather than isolated."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to give aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the defining carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the Strecker reaction class (Strecker, 1850). Formaldehyde supplies the eventual glycine methylene carbon, and the added cyanide supplies the carboxyl carbon. Direct interception of photogenerated methanimine remains a kinetic competition, not an established high-yield geological process."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7.5-9.5; HCN/CN- available; cyanohydrin formation remains reversible."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the formaldehyde carbonyl, and protonation of the resulting alkoxide yields glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is established chemistry and is integral to HCN-based prebiotic homologation networks (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). Glycolonitrile can act as a chemically real carbonyl-cyanide reservoir. It is not assumed to be indefinitely stable against dissociation, hydrolysis or further photochemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Ammonia-mediated conversion of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonia-containing solution, approximately pH 8-10; cyanohydrin, carbonyl, imine and cyanide species interconvert."}, "mechanism": "Net cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion occurs through cyanohydrin dissociation, carbonyl-imine exchange and cyanide recapture. Uncatalyzed displacement of hydroxide from an alcohol is not assumed.", "reasoning": "This represents the cyanohydrin-entry branch of the Strecker reaction manifold, not a separate elementary substitution mechanism. It is chemically justified when glycolonitrile accumulates before ammonia exposure. The distinct pathway is therefore an alternative reservoir and mixing history, not evidence for a wholly independent glycine-synthesis family."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions for acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-430 K", "pressure": "Approximately 20-200 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Locally acidic fluid, approximately pH 2-4; prolonged residence may be necessary; organic material must avoid the hottest vent fluid."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation facilitates water addition; proton transfers and tautomerization generate the amide.", "reasoning": "Nitrile-to-amide hydration is an established first stage of Strecker-product hydrolysis. The exact rate at natural acidity and concentration is not established here; many laboratory hydrolyses use stronger acid. Transport and dilution of surface-produced aminoacetonitrile are additional unresolved bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions for acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-440 K", "pressure": "Approximately 20-200 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 2-4; ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions; cool products after sufficient hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl, followed by tetrahedral-intermediate collapse and release of ammonia or ammonium.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis completes the classical conversion of an aminonitrile skeleton into an amino acid. It can be slower than nitrile hydration and is a principal kinetic bottleneck. Natural residence times, actual acid activity and competing glycine degradation must be assessed before assigning a yield."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Carbon-dioxide capture and hydantoin formation", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonium/inorganic-carbon solution, approximately pH 7-9; dissolved CO2 is replenished by its bicarbonate equilibrium."}, "mechanism": "Aminoacetonitrile captures CO2 to form a carbamate-derived intermediate, followed by cyclization and rearrangement through the Bucherer-Bergs reaction manifold to hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin formation from carbonyl compounds, cyanide and ammonium carbonate is established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934). The displayed aminonitrile-plus-CO2 reaction is a net representation of its downstream cyclization chemistry. Hydantoin can sequester the glycine skeleton, but its formation requires suitable concentrations and its later hydrolysis is harder than formation."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions for acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "410-450 K", "pressure": "Approximately 30-200 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Locally acidic aqueous compartment, approximately pH 2-3; extended heating; avoid drying that favors ring closure."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic opening of the cyclic imide produces N-carbamoylglycine, represented by its neutral hydantoic-acid formula.", "reasoning": "Hydantoins are established hydrolysable amino-acid precursors in Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. Applying acidic hydrolysis at these geological conditions is a conditional extrapolation: practical laboratory procedures can use stronger acid or alkali. Slow ring opening and reverse cyclization are important limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydantoic-acid hydrolysis and decarbamoylation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions for acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "420-450 K", "pressure": "Approximately 30-200 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3 in a locally acidic fluid; prolonged heating may be needed; neutral formulas summarize actual protonated products."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis removes the carbamoyl group. The resulting carbamic-acid-derived fragment decomposes to ammonia and CO2, leaving glycine.", "reasoning": "This completes the chemically established hydantoin-to-amino-acid transformation while explicitly retaining the ring-opened intermediate. The proposed natural implementation is uncertain because urea-bond cleavage can require harsh conditions. The CO2 incorporated during hydantoin formation is released again and does not become either carbon atom of glycine."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of glyoxylic acid", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Accessible metallic nickel sites in native Ni-Fe alloy, such as awaruite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-420 K", "pressure": "Approximately 50-200 bar with substantial hydrogen partial pressure", "catalyst": "Native Ni-Fe alloy with active metallic nickel sites", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-9; ammonia-rich anoxic water; surface passivation and sulfide poisoning must be limited."}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylic acid reversibly condenses with ammonia to form an imine-derived species. Surface-activated hydrogen reduces its carbon-nitrogen double bond, giving glycine.", "reasoning": "Reductive amination is established chemistry, but the performance of a natural awaruite surface for this exact substrate and condition set is a speculative geochemical extrapolation here. Nickel is proposed specifically for hydrogen activation, not merely because a mineral is present. Competing carbonyl reduction to glycolate, glyoxylate hydration and catalyst poisoning could prevent useful selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Native-iron-driven reductive amination of glyoxylic acid", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_016", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_018", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native Fe(0), a consumed reducing mineral also represented explicitly as mol_016"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-410 K", "pressure": "Approximately 20-100 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6-8 with buffering and sufficient total ammonia; Fe(0) oxidizes to Fe(II). The inferred net stoichiometry consumes two proton equivalents per glycine."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia and glyoxylic acid establish an imine-derived intermediate. Iron corrosion supplies reducing equivalents, directly or through locally generated hydrogen, that can reduce this intermediate to glycine.", "reasoning": "This is a speculative mineral-specific implementation of reductive amination, not a claim of demonstrated selectivity under the stated conditions. Fe(0) is a stoichiometric reductant rather than a catalyst, and its oxidation product is included for redox accounting. Native-iron availability, passivation, competing hydrogen evolution and preferential glyoxylate-to-glycolate reduction are critical uncertainties. It differs from rxn_013 in its electron source and dependence on mineral consumption."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Methanol-derived imine-entry Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Photochemically generated formaldehyde enters the imine branch, is trapped by cyanide, and undergoes sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis. The methanol photochemical entry is conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Methanol-derived cyanohydrin-entry Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Methanol-derived formaldehyde is first stored as glycolonitrile. Subsequent ammonia exposure accesses aminoacetonitrile, followed by hydrolysis to glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Direct capture of HCN-derived methanimine", "description": "A first HCN carbon is photoreduced to methanimine and intercepted by additional cyanide before hydrolysis. The resulting aminoacetonitrile is hydrolysed to glycine; successful imine interception is conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "HCN photoreduction through a glycolonitrile reservoir", "description": "Photogenerated methanimine hydrolyses to formaldehyde, which captures additional cyanide. Ammonia-mediated conversion of the glycolonitrile reservoir gives the aminonitrile that is hydrolysed to glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Methanol-derived imine entry with hydantoin sequestration", "description": "The methanol-to-imine entry supplies aminoacetonitrile, which captures inorganic carbon as hydantoin rather than undergoing immediate nitrile hydration. Ring opening and decarbamoylation release glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Methanol-derived cyanohydrin entry with hydantoin sequestration", "description": "A glycolonitrile reservoir formed from methanol-derived formaldehyde is converted to aminoacetonitrile. Hydantoin formation then precedes hydrolytic release of glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Direct HCN-derived imine capture followed by hydantoin formation", "description": "Cyanide interception of photogenerated methanimine produces aminoacetonitrile. Inorganic-carbon capture stores the skeleton as hydantoin, whose subsequent hydrolysis gives glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "HCN-derived cyanohydrin reservoir followed by hydantoin formation", "description": "HCN photoreduction supplies formaldehyde through methanimine hydrolysis. Sequential glycolonitrile and aminoacetonitrile formation leads to hydantoin sequestration and eventual glycine release.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Hydrogen-fed mineral-catalysed glyoxylate reductive amination", "description": "Supplied glyoxylic acid and ammonia are reductively coupled using hydrogen on a proposed native Ni-Fe catalytic surface. This bypasses cyanide and hydantoin chemistry; the mineral-specific implementation remains speculative.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Sacrificial-native-iron glyoxylate reductive amination", "description": "Supplied glyoxylic acid and ammonia are reductively coupled using electrons from consumed native iron. Fe(II) formation distinguishes this conditional route from hydrogen-fed catalytic reduction.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_014"], "key_intermediates": []}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_015", "mol_020"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_003", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_009", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_013", "rxn_014"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_004", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_013", "rxn_014"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_006"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_011", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_020", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_014"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 479152.8124809265, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:25:41.547840", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glycine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct ten distinct feedstock-and-branch sequences: eight traverse the experimentally grounded Strecker/cyanohydrin reaction manifold, with either direct nitrile hydrolysis or hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis; two use reductive amination of glyoxylic acid with different electron sources. These are ten network pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic mechanisms. Methanol photodehydrogenation and mineral-specific reductive amination are conditional, partly speculative branches, identified in their reaction reasoning. Methanol, HCN, ammonia, sulfide, inorganic carbon, hydrogen and glyoxylic acid are supplied feedstocks; their environmental production is outside this network. Surface-generated aminonitriles or hydantoins require transfer into heated aqueous compartments for the hydrolysis conditions proposed here, and successful transport is not established. Twenty molecule nodes and fourteen reaction nodes retain the principal hydrolysis intermediates explicitly. All reactions exclude molecular oxygen. Organic formulas use neutral reference species; actual aqueous protonation and hydration depend on pH. The schema has no coproduct role, so terminal sulfur and Fe(II) coproducts are labeled intermediate for bookkeeping only. No end-to-end yield or naturally operating integrated network is claimed.\n\nMOLECULES (20 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH4O (Methanol) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CHN (HCN) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H3N (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_007: H2 (Hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_008: CH2O (Formaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: CH3N (Formaldimine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C2H3NO (Glycolonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C2H4N2 (Aminoacetonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C2H6N2O (Glycinamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C3H4N2O2 (Hydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C3H6N2O3 (Hydantoic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C2H2O3 (Glyoxylic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_016: Fe (Native iron) [starting]\n  - mol_017: H+ (Proton) [starting]\n  - mol_018: Fe+2 (Ferrous ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C2H5NO2 (Glycine) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (14 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001 -> mol_008, mol_007 (Anoxic partial photodehydrogenation of methanol) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_002, mol_005 -> mol_009, mol_019 (Sulfide-driven photoreduction of HCN to transient methanimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_009, mol_004 -> mol_008, mol_003 (Methanimine hydrolysis to formaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_008, mol_003 -> mol_009, mol_004 (Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_009, mol_002 -> mol_011 (Cyanide capture of methanimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_008, mol_002 -> mol_010 (Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_010, mol_003 -> mol_011, mol_004 (Ammonia-mediated conversion of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_011, mol_004 -> mol_012 (Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_009: mol_012, mol_004 -> mol_020, mol_003 (Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_010: mol_011, mol_006 -> mol_013 (Carbon-dioxide capture and hydantoin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_013, mol_004 -> mol_014 (Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_012: mol_014, mol_004 -> mol_020, mol_003, mol_006 (Hydantoic-acid hydrolysis and decarbamoylation) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_013: mol_015, mol_003, mol_007 -> mol_020, mol_004 (Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of glyoxylic acid) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_014: mol_015, mol_003, mol_016, mol_017 -> mol_020, mol_018, mol_004 (Native-iron-driven reductive amination of glyoxylic acid) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Methanol-derived imine-entry Strecker hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P2: Methanol-derived cyanohydrin-entry Strecker hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P3: Direct capture of HCN-derived methanimine (4 steps)\n  - P4: HCN photoreduction through a glycolonitrile reservoir (6 steps)\n  - P5: Methanol-derived imine entry with hydantoin sequestration (6 steps)\n  - P6: Methanol-derived cyanohydrin entry with hydantoin sequestration (6 steps)\n  - P7: Direct HCN-derived imine capture followed by hydantoin formation (5 steps)\n  - P8: HCN-derived cyanohydrin reservoir followed by hydantoin formation (7 steps)\n  - P9: Hydrogen-fed mineral-catalysed glyoxylate reductive amination (1 steps)\n  - P10: Sacrificial-native-iron glyoxylate reductive amination (1 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 14 reactions | 12 balanced as written | 2 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_002 balanceable: 8 CHN + 8 H2S -> 8 CH3N + S8\n  - rxn_014 balanceable: C2H2O3 + H3N + Fe + 2 H+ -> C2H5NO2 + Fe+2 + H2O\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conse