{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:28:58.210183", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Glycine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.71\n- Feedback: The network has high structural and stoichiometric validity: all 20 molecule nodes participate in a connected graph, identifiers are consistent, and the deterministic check establishes conservation for every reaction. rxn_002 requires 8 HCN + 8 H2S -> 8 CH3N + S8; the coefficient eight reflects the sulfur allotrope and is not anomalous. rxn_014 requires 2 H+. Neither reaction is a conservation violation. Terminal sulfur and Fe(II) bookkeeping labels do not invalidate connectivity. The Strecker, cyanohydrin and hydrolysis chemistry provides credible conditional routes to glycine, and the hydantoin branch is chemically plausible. However, the submission provides only broad environment labels, without the UV spectrum, catalyst identities, concentrations, pH, temperature, pressure or residence times needed to verify operation. Methanol photodehydrogenation and mineral-mediated reductive amination remain particularly underdetermined. Surface-to-heated-water transport and intermediate survival are acknowledged but not physically established. Anoxia is not intrinsically incompatible with the proposed chemistry, and supplied feedstock origins are correctly outside the evaluation boundary. No research findings or reaction-specific citations were provided for a separate literature-alignment assessment. Problematic reaction lists identify evidentiary, selectivity or operating-condition concerns, not atom-balance failures. The explicit uncertainties prevent overclaiming but do not resolve the missing enabling conditions. Overall confidence therefore remains below the 0.9 network approval threshold; neither pathway multiplicity nor the number or order of environments contributes to the score.\n- Issues: ['The Strecker sequence and glycine-forming hydrolyses are chemically credible. The principal weakness is methanol photodehydrogenation without a specified usable UV spectrum or photocatalyst. Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation is reversible, with transient methanimine competing against hydration and other condensation products. Transport and hydrolysis operating conditions remain unspecified.', 'Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation provides a credible entry into the Strecker manifold. Glycolonitrile-to-aminoacetonitrile conversion is plausible as an ammonia-dependent equilibrium sequence, not necessarily direct displacement of hydroxyl. The uncertain methanol photochemistry remains an upstream bottleneck, and heated aqueous transfer alone does not establish effective hydrolysis.', 'Sulfide-assisted HCN photoreduction is a plausible source of transient methanimine, and cyanide addition gives the correct glycine precursor. However, methanimine capture must compete successfully with hydrolysis and further photoreduction. The specified surface environment does not establish that selectivity, and the subsequent hydrolysis conditions and transfer remain unresolved.', 'This is the strongest conditional pathway because methanimine hydrolysis is used productively rather than requiring immediate interception, followed by well-founded formaldehyde cyanohydrin chemistry. Its advantage is mechanistic, not its number of steps. Photochemical operating conditions, ammonia-driven conversion of the reservoir, transport and complete hydrolysis still require substantiation.', 'Hydantoin sequestration is a chemically plausible extension of aminonitrile chemistry, and rxn_010 is balanced without an additional net reagent. Nevertheless, CO2 availability alone does not establish effective cyclization. This pathway combines uncertain methanol photochemistry with an unspecified hydantoin formation window and demanding ring-opening/decarbamoylation conditions.', 'The cyanohydrin entry and hydantoin endpoint form a coherent chemical sequence. However, the proposed reservoir and sequestration functions do not themselves establish efficient conversion or survival during transfer. Methanol activation remains conditional, while hydantoin formation and complete hydrolysis require conditions more specific than the stated environment labels.', 'The proposed transformations connect HCN-derived methanimine to glycine without a conservation defect. Two important kinetic assumptions remain: selective cyanide capture of photogenerated methanimine and productive hydantoin formation from the resulting aminonitrile. Hydantoin could aid storage, but that benefit and its subsequent complete hydrolysis are not established here.', 'This is the strongest hydantoin variant because it accommodates methanimine hydrolysis and uses credible cyanohydrin chemistry before sequestration. Its limitations concern conversion selectivity and operating conditions, not pathway length. In particular, hydantoin formation, transport and hydrolysis through hydantoic acid have not been shown to function as an integrated sequence.', 'The net reductive amination is balanced and chemically reasonable, but hydrogen does not establish a kinetically effective reduction system by itself. No mineral catalyst, hydrogen pressure, pH or temperature is specified. Selective imine reduction must also compete with direct glyoxylate reduction to glycolate and other losses.', 'Sacrificial Fe(0) supplies the required two electrons, and the deterministic balance is satisfied with two protons. The concern is selective reaction kinetics rather than conservation or the choice of supplied iron feedstock. Iron corrosion, hydrogen evolution and passivation can compete with reduction, while pH must permit sufficient imine formation from glyoxylate and ammonia.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct ten explicit reaction-sequence pathways while distinguishing chemical plausibility from demonstrated operation. P1-P8 are related Strecker/Bucherer-Bergs route variants: supplied formaldehyde or photochemically generated formaldehyde, imine capture or cyanohydrin-mediated entry, and direct nitrile hydrolysis or hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis. They are not eight independent amino-acid synthesis mechanisms. P9 uses mineral-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate; P10 instead forms and reduces its oxime. Methanol photodehydrogenation and unspecified hydrogenation catalysts are excluded. The conditions below are proposed experimental screening windows, not claimed literature-validated yields or residence times. The strongest evidence concerns Strecker chemistry; dilute aqueous hydantoin formation and release, photochemical selectivity, and mineral-driven reductive amination remain conditional. Starting materials are boundary inputs, and their geological production is not established by this network. Photochemistry occurs only in a thin illuminated surface compartment; subsequent reactions occur in shaded solutions or opaque subsurface pores. Surface-to-hydrothermal pathways require cool downward recharge through approximately 100-500 m of coastal geothermal fractures, followed by residence in a hotter reaction pocket. Transfer within approximately one day requires connected-fracture velocities of approximately 0.001-0.006 m/s; this is a transport requirement, not an established field observation. Aminonitrile or hydantoin, rather than free methanimine, must survive that transfer. All solutions are anoxic, and reduced iron is compatible with the specified settings. Unless otherwise stated, pH refers to a cooled 298 K aliquot. Neutral molecular formulas provide acid-base-normalized bookkeeping; hydrogen ions and ferrous ions are explicit where required for metal-reduction balance. Terminal sulfur and iron products use the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available. No integrated ten-pathway prebiotic system is claimed to have been demonstrated.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanimine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH3N/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)acetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydantoic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"mackinawite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) disulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyrite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"native iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen(1+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proton\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe+2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic photoreduction and hydrolysis to formaldehyde equivalents\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Cu(I)-cyanide complexes generated from CuCN in excess cyanide; photoredox catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar; gas-restricted, anoxic compartment\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"0.1-1 mM total copper introduced as CuCN with excess cyanide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: 10-50 mM total HCN/CN-, 5-50 mM total H2S/HS-, pH 7.0-8.5, and an optical path of 1-3 mm. Use 254 +/- 10 nm irradiation at 0.1-1 mW/cm2 for a time-course of 0.5-12 h. This narrow-band illumination is a laboratory proxy, not a claim about the ancient solar spectrum. Measure copper precipitation, light attenuation, sulfur speciation, and formaldehyde equivalents. Stop illumination before extensive onward reduction of cyanohydrins. Transfer products immediately into shade; do not transport or accumulate free methanimine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV-driven copper-cyanide photoredox chemistry supplies reducing equivalents from sulfide. Reduction of HCN gives a transient imine that hydrolyzes to formaldehyde and ammonia. The node represents the coupled net process, not selective isolation of its transient imine. Elemental sulfur is the normalized oxidized-sulfur endpoint; polysulfides can intervene.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanosulfidic photochemistry has experimental precedent for generating aldehyde-derived prebiotic building blocks (Patel et al., 2015). Unlike unspecified methanol photochemistry, this edge identifies both a usable laboratory UV band and a photocatalyst. Nevertheless, formaldehyde is strongly hydrated and can be captured immediately as glycolonitrile. Copper sulfide precipitation, polysulfide absorption, and further photoreduction are genuine limitations. The stated window is a testable implementation, not a reported selective formaldehyde yield; broadband-solar operation and sulfur-product partitioning require verification.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of methanimine from formaldehyde and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use 1-10 mM formaldehyde equivalents and 0.1-1 M total NH3/NH4+ at pH 9.0-10.0 in shaded, gas-restricted pores or pools. Supply formaldehyde gradually into an ammonia/cyanide solution rather than aging a concentrated formaldehyde-ammonia mixture. Couple directly to rxn_003 with 10-100 mM total cyanide. Sample over 0.5-24 h; no separate methanimine storage or isolation is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the carbonyl to form a carbinolamine, which exchanges with methanimine and water. Cyanide capture in the following reaction can draw on a small transient imine population.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imine formation followed by cyanide addition is the conventional Strecker mechanism (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Water disfavors substantial accumulation of free methanimine, and formaldehyde hydration and hexamethylenetetramine formation compete. This node therefore denotes an in-situ intermediate, not a stable aqueous feedstock. Excess ammonia and immediate cyanide trapping are enabling conditions, but they do not establish a quantitative imine concentration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of methanimine to aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Operate in the same shaded solution as rxn_002: pH 9.0-10.0, total cyanide 10-100 mM, total ammonia 0.1-1 M, and formaldehyde equivalents 1-10 mM. Follow aminonitrile formation over 0.5-24 h. Limit exposure to UV and avoid prolonged alkaline aging that promotes unrelated cyanide chemistry. No mineral catalyst is required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to give aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the glycine Strecker route (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). It is better supported than assuming selective capture of an independently transported photochemical imine. Competition with imine hydrolysis remains important, so productive operation requires coupled formation and trapping in one compartment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use 1-20 mM formaldehyde equivalents, 10-100 mM total cyanide, and pH 8.0-9.5. Examine equilibration over 0.1-24 h. Keep the solution shaded after any photochemical feed-generation stage. For a reservoir-dominated stage, avoid adding the large ammonia excess specified for rxn_005 until cyanohydrin formation has been assessed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds reversibly to formaldehyde, and proton transfer produces glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin chemistry provides a well-founded connection between aldehyde supply and Strecker chemistry (Miller, 1957). Glycolonitrile also occurs within experimentally investigated cyanosulfidic networks (Patel et al., 2015). It can hold formaldehyde equivalents without requiring free imine accumulation, but its formation does not by itself establish long-term storage or subsequent conversion efficiency.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of the glycolonitrile reservoir to aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"305-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use 1-20 mM glycolonitrile equivalents, 0.1-1 M total NH3/NH4+, and pH 9.0-10.0; retain a 10-100 mM total cyanide pool. Keep the mixture shaded and gas-restricted. Screen 6-72 h while measuring both cyanohydrin and aminonitrile. Ammonia-rich concentrations require a concentrating environment and are not assumed for dilute open seawater.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation releases formaldehyde and cyanide. Ammonia competes for formaldehyde through carbinolamine/imine formation, followed by cyanide recapture. The net reaction is not assumed to be direct displacement of hydroxyl by ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is an operational reservoir-entry variant of the Strecker manifold, consistent with the mechanistic framework discussed by Miller (1957). It is not an independent elementary amination mechanism. A substantial ammonia activity and favorable coupled equilibria are required; formaldehyde condensation products and cyanide side reactions must be quantified rather than assuming complete conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-promoted hydration of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by an acidic fluid; acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic-pore screen: 0.1-10 mM aminoacetonitrile and 0.03-0.3 M strong-acid equivalents, corresponding approximately to pH 0.5-1.5 before heating. Screen 6-168 h. Measure glycinamide and glycine together because rxn_007 can proceed before glycinamide accumulates. Deliver the aminonitrile through a cool, shaded recharge branch before heating; do not assume survival during prolonged hot transit.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Protonation activates the nitrile for water addition. Subsequent proton transfers and tautomerization produce glycinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis is an established completion step of Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850); the amide is the expected hydrolysis intermediate. Specifying acidity, temperature, and residence-time screening addresses the insufficiency of merely invoking heated water. The chosen acid-rich hydrothermal pocket is a conditional setting, not a claim that ordinary seawater hydrolyzes the nitrile efficiently. Deamination and other decomposition must be checked experimentally.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by an acidic fluid; acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use the same 0.03-0.3 M strong-acid-equivalent solution as rxn_006, with glycinamide in the 0.1-10 mM range. Screen total hot residence times of 1-7 days for the combined nitrile-to-acid sequence. The two hydrolysis-node time windows are not necessarily additive. Cool the effluent after conversion and monitor glycine loss. The ammonia product is predominantly ammonium under these conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage gives glycine and ammonia, expressed in neutral acid-base-normalized form.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is ordinary amide hydrolysis within the established Strecker endpoint chemistry (Strecker, 1850). It needs no speculative mineral catalyst. Complete nitrile disappearance is not sufficient evidence of glycine production: residual glycinamide and degradation products must also be measured. The hot residence time must be shorter than significant glycine destruction under the actual fluid composition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs conversion of aminoacetonitrile to hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar total pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed aqueous screen: 1-10 mM aminoacetonitrile, 0.05-0.5 M dissolved inorganic carbon, 0.1-0.5 M total NH3/NH4+, and 1-20 mM residual total cyanide at pH 8.0-9.0. Screen 1-7 days in shaded, gas-restricted pores. Use a CO2-bearing atmosphere or carbonate-rich inflow consistent with the measured pH and dissolved-carbon concentration; do not assume atmospheric CO2 alone supplies the required loading. No organic cosolvent is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbamylation of the aminonitrile is followed by five-membered-ring formation and rearrangement to hydantoin. The net molecular transformation incorporates carbon dioxide without requiring an additional net nitrogen reagent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile and cyanohydrin entry into hydantoin synthesis is established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry, reviewed by Ware (1950). This supports a chemically meaningful alternative to immediate nitrile hydrolysis. However, many preparative implementations use concentrated reagents or cosolvents. Efficient operation in the stated dilute aqueous geochemical window is not established. Hydantoin is therefore a conditional sequestration branch, not an assumed quantitative storage solution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions in an acid-rich hydrothermal pocket\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423-453 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Conditional release screen: 0.1-10 mM hydantoin and 0.1-1 M strong-acid equivalents, approximately pH 0-1 before heating. Follow 1-14 days with repeated measurements of hydantoin, hydantoic acid, and glycine. Such acid-rich fluids are a restrictive requirement, not a generic vent condition. Transfer hydantoin through cool shaded fractures before the hot release stage. No yield is assumed from temperature alone.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-assisted water addition to a ring carbonyl and cleavage of the corresponding acyl-nitrogen bond produce the open-chain ureido acid, hydantoic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is established laboratory chemistry, but its resistance to hydrolysis makes release an important bottleneck (Ware, 1950). The stated temperature and acidity define a testable release window rather than asserting that mild heated water is sufficient. Rates in the actual salt-rich fluid, competing ring closure, and glycine survival must be established before this branch can be considered operational.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoic acid decarbamoylation to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions in an acid-rich hydrothermal pocket\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423-453 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue in the 0.1-1 M strong-acid-equivalent fluid used for rxn_009. Screen a combined hydantoin-release residence time of 1-14 days rather than assigning independent consecutive holding periods. A flowing pocket can remove dissolved inorganic carbon and ammonium, but liquid-water pressure is retained. Measure re-formed hydantoin and glycine decomposition; cool collected effluent promptly.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the ureido group releases glycine and carbamic-acid equivalents, which decompose to ammonia and carbon dioxide. All three final products are included in the net reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Complete hydantoin-to-amino-acid hydrolysis requires removal of the carbamoyl group, not merely ring opening (Ware, 1950). This explicit step avoids treating hydantoic acid as if it were already glycine. Its geochemical rate and competition with cyclization are unresolved in the proposed fluid window, so P5-P8 remain more conditional than the direct Strecker-hydrolysis pathways.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional FeS/sulfide-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Freshly precipitated mackinawite; explicitly consumed mineral reactant rather than an unspecified catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"363-383 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar in an anoxic, sulfide-retaining pore system\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: 0.1-5 mM glyoxylate, 0.1-0.5 M total NH3/NH4+, 10-100 mM dissolved H2S/HS-, and 50-200 mM FeS expressed as solid formula-unit equivalents; pH 6.5-8.0 before heating. Use fresh, characterized mackinawite and follow 1-7 days. Quantify glycine, glycolate, pyrite formation, hydrogen evolution, and remaining glyoxylate. No externally supplied H2 is treated as a sufficient reductant by itself.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms a transient glyoxylate imine. Sulfide incorporation into FeS and formation of disulfide units in pyrite provide the formal two-electron reducing capacity needed to reduce the imine. Pyrite formation and water production are included explicitly in the net equation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mineral-assisted reductive amination has reaction-family precedent in origins-of-life chemistry (Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 2003). This particular pyrite-coupled implementation is conditional: atom and electron balance do not prove useful glycine selectivity. Direct carbonyl reduction, sulfide addition, hydrogen escape, and mineral passivation can compete. Including mineral identity, loading, ammonia activity, pressure, and a residence-time screen makes the hypothesis testable, but neither target yield nor operation at these exact conditions is claimed as established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Capture of glyoxylate as its oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_019\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use 1-10 mM glyoxylate and 5-50 mM total hydroxylamine at pH 4.5-6.0. Screen 1-24 h in a shaded compartment. Allow oxime formation before contacting native iron, since iron can otherwise consume hydroxylamine directly. The oxime node does not specify an E/Z composition; determine that composition experimentally.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine adds to the aldehyde group of glyoxylate, followed by dehydration to form the corresponding oxime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxime-mediated reductive amination has direct prebiotic-network precedent using hydroxylamine and metallic iron (Muchowska et al., 2019). Preforming an oxime avoids relying solely on a very small ammonia-derived imine population during metal reduction. Hydroxylamine supply is nevertheless a major boundary assumption: its availability and persistence are not established here, and its concentration cannot be inferred from anoxia alone.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction of glyoxylate oxime to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_019\",\n        \"mol_020\",\n        \"mol_021\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_022\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Fresh native iron or exposed metallic iron surfaces; stoichiometric electron donor, not a catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-373 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: 1-5 mM preformed oxime and 5-20 molar equivalents of accessible Fe(0), with pH 2.5-4.0 maintained by acidic inflow. Cumulative proton supply must cover the oxime reduction plus competing iron corrosion; initial pH alone does not specify reductant capacity. Screen 4-48 h using measured iron surface area. Track glycine, regenerated glyoxylate, glycolate, dissolved Fe2+, and H2. Use recently exposed metal because oxide or sulfide coatings can suppress electron transfer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A four-electron reduction cleaves the oxime N-O bond and reduces the carbon-nitrogen unsaturation. Oxidation of metallic iron to Fe2+ supplies the electrons; proton uptake and water formation complete the atom and charge balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydroxylamine/metallic-iron conversion of keto-acid-derived intermediates to amino acids has experimental precedent (Muchowska et al., 2019). This route addresses the selectivity weakness of assigning Fe(0) directly to unspecified ammonia reductive amination: the nitrogen-bearing oxime is formed first. However, the stated operating window is a proposed implementation rather than a reproduced literature protocol. Corrosion, passivation, oxime hydrolysis, and the geological scarcity of accessible native iron remain limitations.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied-formaldehyde imine-entry Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Boundary-supplied formaldehyde forms a transient imine with ammonia, which is trapped by cyanide. Aminoacetonitrile is then hydrolyzed through glycinamide. Imine formation and capture occur together; only the aminonitrile is transferred to the heated compartment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied-formaldehyde cyanohydrin-entry Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde is first captured as glycolonitrile. Ammonia-dependent equilibration transfers material into aminoacetonitrile, followed by nitrile and amide hydrolysis. This is a reservoir-entry variant of P1, not an independent elementary amination mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical C1 supply followed by imine-entry Strecker synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanosulfidic photochemistry generates formaldehyde equivalents, which enter the coupled imine/cyanide-capture sequence after shading and adjustment of ammonia activity. This adds a carbon-feedstock-generation module to P1; selective availability of the formaldehyde pool must be demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical C1 supply followed by cyanohydrin-entry Strecker synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"Photochemically generated formaldehyde is captured as glycolonitrile, followed by ammonia-dependent aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis. This route accommodates cyanohydrin capture rather than requiring isolation of free formaldehyde or photogenerated methanimine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied-formaldehyde imine entry with hydantoin sequestration\",\n      \"description\": \"Imine-entry Strecker chemistry produces aminoacetonitrile, which incorporates inorganic carbon to form hydantoin. Ring opening and decarbamoylation subsequently release glycine. Both aqueous hydantoin formation and complete release are conditional bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied-formaldehyde cyanohydrin entry with hydantoin sequestration\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolonitrile supplies aminoacetonitrile under ammonia-rich conditions. Hydantoin formation provides a chemically distinct alternative to immediate aminonitrile hydrolysis, followed by demanding ring-opening and decarbamoylation steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical formaldehyde supply with imine entry and hydantoin release\",\n      \"description\": \"The photochemical feed-generation module precedes imine-entry aminonitrile synthesis, hydantoin formation, and complete hydrolysis. This combines the uncertainties of photochemical selectivity and hydantoin release and is not presented as an experimentally integrated sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical cyanohydrin entry with hydantoin sequestration and release\",\n      \"description\": \"Photogenerated formaldehyde equivalents pass through glycolonitrile and aminoacetonitrile before hydantoin formation. The route avoids transport of transient imines, but useful storage, transfer survival, and subsequent glycine release all require measurement.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional mineral-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate and ammonia produce glycine in a single net reductive-amination node coupled to FeS/sulfide conversion to pyrite. The imine is an in-situ mechanistic intermediate rather than a transported species. This is a testable, less-established branch, not a demonstrated selective process at the proposed conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate oxime formation followed by native-iron reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydroxylamine captures glyoxylate as an oxime before contact with native iron. Acid-supported four-electron reduction then yields glycine. This avoids requiring selective reduction of an ammonia-derived imine but introduces hydroxylamine supply and accessible native iron as explicit geochemical bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "Construct ten explicit reaction-sequence pathways while distinguishing chemical plausibility from demonstrated operation. P1-P8 are related Strecker/Bucherer-Bergs route variants: supplied formaldehyde or photochemically generated formaldehyde, imine capture or cyanohydrin-mediated entry, and direct nitrile hydrolysis or hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis. They are not eight independent amino-acid synthesis mechanisms. P9 uses mineral-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate; P10 instead forms and reduces its oxime. Methanol photodehydrogenation and unspecified hydrogenation catalysts are excluded. The conditions below are proposed experimental screening windows, not claimed literature-validated yields or residence times. The strongest evidence concerns Strecker chemistry; dilute aqueous hydantoin formation and release, photochemical selectivity, and mineral-driven reductive amination remain conditional. Starting materials are boundary inputs, and their geological production is not established by this network. Photochemistry occurs only in a thin illuminated surface compartment; subsequent reactions occur in shaded solutions or opaque subsurface pores. Surface-to-hydrothermal pathways require cool downward recharge through approximately 100-500 m of coastal geothermal fractures, followed by residence in a hotter reaction pocket. Transfer within approximately one day requires connected-fracture velocities of approximately 0.001-0.006 m/s; this is a transport requirement, not an established field observation. Aminonitrile or hydantoin, rather than free methanimine, must survive that transfer. All solutions are anoxic, and reduced iron is compatible with the specified settings. Unless otherwise stated, pH refers to a cooled 298 K aliquot. Neutral molecular formulas provide acid-base-normalized bookkeeping; hydrogen ions and ferrous ions are explicit where required for metal-reduction balance. Terminal sulfur and iron products use the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available. No integrated ten-pathway prebiotic system is claimed to have been demonstrated.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CH3N", "iupac_name": "methanimine", "common_name": "formaldimine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH3N/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetamide", "common_name": "glycinamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H4N2O2", "iupac_name": "imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H6N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)acetic acid", "common_name": "hydantoic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "FeS", "iupac_name": "iron(II) sulfide", "common_name": "mackinawite", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "FeS2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) disulfide", "common_name": "pyrite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C2H3NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydrogen(1+)", "common_name": "proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Cyanosulfidic photoreduction and hydrolysis to formaldehyde equivalents", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)-cyanide complexes generated from CuCN in excess cyanide; photoredox catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar; gas-restricted, anoxic compartment", "catalyst": "0.1-1 mM total copper introduced as CuCN with excess cyanide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 10-50 mM total HCN/CN-, 5-50 mM total H2S/HS-, pH 7.0-8.5, and an optical path of 1-3 mm. Use 254 +/- 10 nm irradiation at 0.1-1 mW/cm2 for a time-course of 0.5-12 h. This narrow-band illumination is a laboratory proxy, not a claim about the ancient solar spectrum. Measure copper precipitation, light attenuation, sulfur speciation, and formaldehyde equivalents. Stop illumination before extensive onward reduction of cyanohydrins. Transfer products immediately into shade; do not transport or accumulate free methanimine."}, "mechanism": "UV-driven copper-cyanide photoredox chemistry supplies reducing equivalents from sulfide. Reduction of HCN gives a transient imine that hydrolyzes to formaldehyde and ammonia. The node represents the coupled net process, not selective isolation of its transient imine. Elemental sulfur is the normalized oxidized-sulfur endpoint; polysulfides can intervene.", "reasoning": "Cyanosulfidic photochemistry has experimental precedent for generating aldehyde-derived prebiotic building blocks (Patel et al., 2015). Unlike unspecified methanol photochemistry, this edge identifies both a usable laboratory UV band and a photocatalyst. Nevertheless, formaldehyde is strongly hydrated and can be captured immediately as glycolonitrile. Copper sulfide precipitation, polysulfide absorption, and further photoreduction are genuine limitations. The stated window is a testable implementation, not a reported selective formaldehyde yield; broadband-solar operation and sulfur-product partitioning require verification."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Reversible formation of methanimine from formaldehyde and ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Use 1-10 mM formaldehyde equivalents and 0.1-1 M total NH3/NH4+ at pH 9.0-10.0 in shaded, gas-restricted pores or pools. Supply formaldehyde gradually into an ammonia/cyanide solution rather than aging a concentrated formaldehyde-ammonia mixture. Couple directly to rxn_003 with 10-100 mM total cyanide. Sample over 0.5-24 h; no separate methanimine storage or isolation is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the carbonyl to form a carbinolamine, which exchanges with methanimine and water. Cyanide capture in the following reaction can draw on a small transient imine population.", "reasoning": "Imine formation followed by cyanide addition is the conventional Strecker mechanism (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Water disfavors substantial accumulation of free methanimine, and formaldehyde hydration and hexamethylenetetramine formation compete. This node therefore denotes an in-situ intermediate, not a stable aqueous feedstock. Excess ammonia and immediate cyanide trapping are enabling conditions, but they do not establish a quantitative imine concentration."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanide capture of methanimine to aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Operate in the same shaded solution as rxn_002: pH 9.0-10.0, total cyanide 10-100 mM, total ammonia 0.1-1 M, and formaldehyde equivalents 1-10 mM. Follow aminonitrile formation over 0.5-24 h. Limit exposure to UV and avoid prolonged alkaline aging that promotes unrelated cyanide chemistry. No mineral catalyst is required."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to give aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the glycine Strecker route (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). It is better supported than assuming selective capture of an independently transported photochemical imine. Competition with imine hydrolysis remains important, so productive operation requires coupled formation and trapping in one compartment."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Use 1-20 mM formaldehyde equivalents, 10-100 mM total cyanide, and pH 8.0-9.5. Examine equilibration over 0.1-24 h. Keep the solution shaded after any photochemical feed-generation stage. For a reservoir-dominated stage, avoid adding the large ammonia excess specified for rxn_005 until cyanohydrin formation has been assessed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds reversibly to formaldehyde, and proton transfer produces glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin chemistry provides a well-founded connection between aldehyde supply and Strecker chemistry (Miller, 1957). Glycolonitrile also occurs within experimentally investigated cyanosulfidic networks (Patel et al., 2015). It can hold formaldehyde equivalents without requiring free imine accumulation, but its formation does not by itself establish long-term storage or subsequent conversion efficiency."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of the glycolonitrile reservoir to aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "305-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Use 1-20 mM glycolonitrile equivalents, 0.1-1 M total NH3/NH4+, and pH 9.0-10.0; retain a 10-100 mM total cyanide pool. Keep the mixture shaded and gas-restricted. Screen 6-72 h while measuring both cyanohydrin and aminonitrile. Ammonia-rich concentrations require a concentrating environment and are not assumed for dilute open seawater."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation releases formaldehyde and cyanide. Ammonia competes for formaldehyde through carbinolamine/imine formation, followed by cyanide recapture. The net reaction is not assumed to be direct displacement of hydroxyl by ammonia.", "reasoning": "This is an operational reservoir-entry variant of the Strecker manifold, consistent with the mechanistic framework discussed by Miller (1957). It is not an independent elementary amination mechanism. A substantial ammonia activity and favorable coupled equilibria are required; formaldehyde condensation products and cyanide side reactions must be quantified rather than assuming complete conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acid-promoted hydration of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by an acidic fluid; acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed acidic-pore screen: 0.1-10 mM aminoacetonitrile and 0.03-0.3 M strong-acid equivalents, corresponding approximately to pH 0.5-1.5 before heating. Screen 6-168 h. Measure glycinamide and glycine together because rxn_007 can proceed before glycinamide accumulates. Deliver the aminonitrile through a cool, shaded recharge branch before heating; do not assume survival during prolonged hot transit."}, "mechanism": "Protonation activates the nitrile for water addition. Subsequent proton transfers and tautomerization produce glycinamide.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis is an established completion step of Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850); the amide is the expected hydrolysis intermediate. Specifying acidity, temperature, and residence-time screening addresses the insufficiency of merely invoking heated water. The chosen acid-rich hydrothermal pocket is a conditional setting, not a claim that ordinary seawater hydrolyzes the nitrile efficiently. Deamination and other decomposition must be checked experimentally."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by an acidic fluid; acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Use the same 0.03-0.3 M strong-acid-equivalent solution as rxn_006, with glycinamide in the 0.1-10 mM range. Screen total hot residence times of 1-7 days for the combined nitrile-to-acid sequence. The two hydrolysis-node time windows are not necessarily additive. Cool the effluent after conversion and monitor glycine loss. The ammonia product is predominantly ammonium under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage gives glycine and ammonia, expressed in neutral acid-base-normalized form.", "reasoning": "This is ordinary amide hydrolysis within the established Strecker endpoint chemistry (Strecker, 1850). It needs no speculative mineral catalyst. Complete nitrile disappearance is not sufficient evidence of glycine production: residual glycinamide and degradation products must also be measured. The hot residence time must be shorter than significant glycine destruction under the actual fluid composition."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs conversion of aminoacetonitrile to hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar total pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed aqueous screen: 1-10 mM aminoacetonitrile, 0.05-0.5 M dissolved inorganic carbon, 0.1-0.5 M total NH3/NH4+, and 1-20 mM residual total cyanide at pH 8.0-9.0. Screen 1-7 days in shaded, gas-restricted pores. Use a CO2-bearing atmosphere or carbonate-rich inflow consistent with the measured pH and dissolved-carbon concentration; do not assume atmospheric CO2 alone supplies the required loading. No organic cosolvent is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Carbamylation of the aminonitrile is followed by five-membered-ring formation and rearrangement to hydantoin. The net molecular transformation incorporates carbon dioxide without requiring an additional net nitrogen reagent.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile and cyanohydrin entry into hydantoin synthesis is established Bucherer-Bergs chemistry, reviewed by Ware (1950). This supports a chemically meaningful alternative to immediate nitrile hydrolysis. However, many preparative implementations use concentrated reagents or cosolvents. Efficient operation in the stated dilute aqueous geochemical window is not established. Hydantoin is therefore a conditional sequestration branch, not an assumed quantitative storage solution."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions in an acid-rich hydrothermal pocket"], "conditions": {"temperature": "423-453 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Conditional release screen: 0.1-10 mM hydantoin and 0.1-1 M strong-acid equivalents, approximately pH 0-1 before heating. Follow 1-14 days with repeated measurements of hydantoin, hydantoic acid, and glycine. Such acid-rich fluids are a restrictive requirement, not a generic vent condition. Transfer hydantoin through cool shaded fractures before the hot release stage. No yield is assumed from temperature alone."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted water addition to a ring carbonyl and cleavage of the corresponding acyl-nitrogen bond produce the open-chain ureido acid, hydantoic acid.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis is established laboratory chemistry, but its resistance to hydrolysis makes release an important bottleneck (Ware, 1950). The stated temperature and acidity define a testable release window rather than asserting that mild heated water is sufficient. Rates in the actual salt-rich fluid, competing ring closure, and glycine survival must be established before this branch can be considered operational."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydantoic acid decarbamoylation to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions in an acid-rich hydrothermal pocket"], "conditions": {"temperature": "423-453 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Aqueous hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue in the 0.1-1 M strong-acid-equivalent fluid used for rxn_009. Screen a combined hydantoin-release residence time of 1-14 days rather than assigning independent consecutive holding periods. A flowing pocket can remove dissolved inorganic carbon and ammonium, but liquid-water pressure is retained. Measure re-formed hydantoin and glycine decomposition; cool collected effluent promptly."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the ureido group releases glycine and carbamic-acid equivalents, which decompose to ammonia and carbon dioxide. All three final products are included in the net reaction.", "reasoning": "Complete hydantoin-to-amino-acid hydrolysis requires removal of the carbamoyl group, not merely ring opening (Ware, 1950). This explicit step avoids treating hydantoic acid as if it were already glycine. Its geochemical rate and competition with cyclization are unresolved in the proposed fluid window, so P5-P8 remain more conditional than the direct Strecker-hydrolysis pathways."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Conditional FeS/sulfide-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004", "mol_016", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_017", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Freshly precipitated mackinawite; explicitly consumed mineral reactant rather than an unspecified catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "363-383 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar in an anoxic, sulfide-retaining pore system", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 0.1-5 mM glyoxylate, 0.1-0.5 M total NH3/NH4+, 10-100 mM dissolved H2S/HS-, and 50-200 mM FeS expressed as solid formula-unit equivalents; pH 6.5-8.0 before heating. Use fresh, characterized mackinawite and follow 1-7 days. Quantify glycine, glycolate, pyrite formation, hydrogen evolution, and remaining glyoxylate. No externally supplied H2 is treated as a sufficient reductant by itself."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms a transient glyoxylate imine. Sulfide incorporation into FeS and formation of disulfide units in pyrite provide the formal two-electron reducing capacity needed to reduce the imine. Pyrite formation and water production are included explicitly in the net equation.", "reasoning": "Mineral-assisted reductive amination has reaction-family precedent in origins-of-life chemistry (Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 2003). This particular pyrite-coupled implementation is conditional: atom and electron balance do not prove useful glycine selectivity. Direct carbonyl reduction, sulfide addition, hydrogen escape, and mineral passivation can compete. Including mineral identity, loading, ammonia activity, pressure, and a residence-time screen makes the hypothesis testable, but neither target yield nor operation at these exact conditions is claimed as established."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Capture of glyoxylate as its oxime", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Use 1-10 mM glyoxylate and 5-50 mM total hydroxylamine at pH 4.5-6.0. Screen 1-24 h in a shaded compartment. Allow oxime formation before contacting native iron, since iron can otherwise consume hydroxylamine directly. The oxime node does not specify an E/Z composition; determine that composition experimentally."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine adds to the aldehyde group of glyoxylate, followed by dehydration to form the corresponding oxime.", "reasoning": "Oxime-mediated reductive amination has direct prebiotic-network precedent using hydroxylamine and metallic iron (Muchowska et al., 2019). Preforming an oxime avoids relying solely on a very small ammonia-derived imine population during metal reduction. Hydroxylamine supply is nevertheless a major boundary assumption: its availability and persistence are not established here, and its concentration cannot be inferred from anoxia alone."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Native-iron reduction of glyoxylate oxime to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_020", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001", "mol_022"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Fresh native iron or exposed metallic iron surfaces; stoichiometric electron donor, not a catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-373 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 1-5 mM preformed oxime and 5-20 molar equivalents of accessible Fe(0), with pH 2.5-4.0 maintained by acidic inflow. Cumulative proton supply must cover the oxime reduction plus competing iron corrosion; initial pH alone does not specify reductant capacity. Screen 4-48 h using measured iron surface area. Track glycine, regenerated glyoxylate, glycolate, dissolved Fe2+, and H2. Use recently exposed metal because oxide or sulfide coatings can suppress electron transfer."}, "mechanism": "A four-electron reduction cleaves the oxime N-O bond and reduces the carbon-nitrogen unsaturation. Oxidation of metallic iron to Fe2+ supplies the electrons; proton uptake and water formation complete the atom and charge balance.", "reasoning": "Hydroxylamine/metallic-iron conversion of keto-acid-derived intermediates to amino acids has experimental precedent (Muchowska et al., 2019). This route addresses the selectivity weakness of assigning Fe(0) directly to unspecified ammonia reductive amination: the nitrogen-bearing oxime is formed first. However, the stated operating window is a proposed implementation rather than a reproduced literature protocol. Corrosion, passivation, oxime hydrolysis, and the geological scarcity of accessible native iron remain limitations."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Supplied-formaldehyde imine-entry Strecker pathway", "description": "Boundary-supplied formaldehyde forms a transient imine with ammonia, which is trapped by cyanide. Aminoacetonitrile is then hydrolyzed through glycinamide. Imine formation and capture occur together; only the aminonitrile is transferred to the heated compartment.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Supplied-formaldehyde cyanohydrin-entry Strecker pathway", "description": "Formaldehyde is first captured as glycolonitrile. Ammonia-dependent equilibration transfers material into aminoacetonitrile, followed by nitrile and amide hydrolysis. This is a reservoir-entry variant of P1, not an independent elementary amination mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Photochemical C1 supply followed by imine-entry Strecker synthesis", "description": "Cyanosulfidic photochemistry generates formaldehyde equivalents, which enter the coupled imine/cyanide-capture sequence after shading and adjustment of ammonia activity. This adds a carbon-feedstock-generation module to P1; selective availability of the formaldehyde pool must be demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Photochemical C1 supply followed by cyanohydrin-entry Strecker synthesis", "description": "Photochemically generated formaldehyde is captured as glycolonitrile, followed by ammonia-dependent aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis. This route accommodates cyanohydrin capture rather than requiring isolation of free formaldehyde or photogenerated methanimine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Supplied-formaldehyde imine entry with hydantoin sequestration", "description": "Imine-entry Strecker chemistry produces aminoacetonitrile, which incorporates inorganic carbon to form hydantoin. Ring opening and decarbamoylation subsequently release glycine. Both aqueous hydantoin formation and complete release are conditional bottlenecks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Supplied-formaldehyde cyanohydrin entry with hydantoin sequestration", "description": "Glycolonitrile supplies aminoacetonitrile under ammonia-rich conditions. Hydantoin formation provides a chemically distinct alternative to immediate aminonitrile hydrolysis, followed by demanding ring-opening and decarbamoylation steps.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Pho