carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO/c1-2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glyoxylic acid oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Aminomalononitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Aminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H5NO4/c4-1(2(5)6)3(7)8/h1H,4H2,(H,5,6)(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of the Strecker imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, mineral-shaded pool or covered pore; pH 8.7-9.5 measured at reaction temperature; total formaldehyde 1-10 mM and total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M. Cyanide for rxn_002 is present from the start at 10-100 mM total concentration. Evaluate the coupled reactions over 6-72 hours. Concentrations denote analytical totals, including hydrated formaldehyde and ammonium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the carbonyl pool through a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to methanimine or its conjugate acid. This node represents a transient intermediate, not an isolatable bulk stock.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The imine-capture manifold is supported by classical Strecker chemistry and studies of amino-acid formation in prebiotic mixtures (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Formaldehyde hydration, imine hydrolysis, and competing formaldehyde-ammonia condensations prevent an assumption of quantitative imine accumulation. Simultaneous cyanide trapping, rather than storage or transport of methanimine, is the enabling design choice. The stated concentration and time ranges are proposed operating windows.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of methanimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same solution and residence interval as rxn_001; pH 8.7-9.5; total cyanide 10-100 mM and total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M. Maintain cyanide in excess over total formaldehyde. Exclude direct UV irradiation and avoid an open, strongly ventilated surface that would rapidly lose HCN.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to produce aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the glycine Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). It requires no speculative mineral redox chemistry. Product formation must nevertheless be measured against competing imine hydrolysis and cyanohydrin formation; the latter is included explicitly as an alternative entry rather than treated as an unexplained loss.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde trapping as glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded aqueous compartment; pH 8.0-9.3; total formaldehyde 1-10 mM and total cyanide 10-100 mM. Initial sampling over 10 minutes to 24 hours. Low ammonia favors identification of the cyanohydrin entry; ammonia enrichment occurs in rxn_004.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to formaldehyde and the resulting alkoxide is protonated, yielding the cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation is established aqueous carbonyl chemistry and is relevant to both classical prebiotic Strecker mixtures and cyanosulfidic chemistry (Miller, 1957; Patel et al., 2015). Glycolonitrile is a chemically justified formaldehyde reservoir, but reservoir formation alone does not demonstrate subsequent aminoacetonitrile production. Its productive release is therefore represented and assessed separately.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in a covered, gas-exchange-limited pore\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 9.0-9.7; glycolonitrile 0.1-10 mM; total ammonia 0.2-0.5 M; total cyanide preferably 10-100 mM; evaluate for 12-168 hours. For photochemical feed, stop irradiation before ammonia enrichment, settle suspended sulfur, and measure residual sulfide and dissolved copper rather than assuming filtration removes them.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycolonitrile reversibly releases formaldehyde and cyanide; ammonia accesses the imine manifold, and cyanide recapture yields aminoacetonitrile. This is a net equilibrium-mediated transformation, not an assumed direct displacement of hydroxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion connects established carbonyl/cyanide/ammonia equilibria (Miller, 1957; Patel et al., 2015). Elevated ammonia activity is essential. The proposed ammonia concentration requires a locally concentrated ammonium-bearing pool, not ordinary seawater. Productive conversion in sulfide- and copper-containing photochemical effluent remains an integration test, so no quantitative flux is assumed from glycolonitrile accumulation alone.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation of HCN to glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Cu(I)-cyanide photocatalyst: CuCN-derived soluble cyanocuprate complexes, 10-100 micromolar total copper\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-313 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic illuminated film 0.1-1 mm thick; pH 7.5-8.5; total cyanide 20-100 mM; total sulfide supplied in limiting pulses of 0.5-2 mM. Use a 254 nm laboratory reference band at 0.05-0.5 mW/cm2 incident irradiance for an initial 1-12 hour screening interval, recording the absorbed photon dose. Natural operation requires an equivalent usable dose from the actual 200-280 nm surface spectrum; photons below 200 nm are not assumed. Dissolved Fe2+ should remain below approximately 50 micromolar through local FeS buffering, despite abundant reduced iron in surrounding rock.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu-mediated photoredox chemistry transfers reducing equivalents from sulfide to HCN. Hydrolysis of the reduced C1 nitrogen intermediate generates formaldehyde equivalents, which excess cyanide traps as glycolonitrile. Sulfide oxidation is represented by elemental sulfur; the equation is a lumped productive branch, not a claim that the complete sulfur product distribution is exclusively S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanosulfidic photochemistry provides experimental precedent for reductive HCN homologation and cyanohydrin-containing product networks (Patel et al., 2015). This design does not require selective interception of free photogenerated methanimine or unsupported methanol photodehydrogenation. Sulfide limitation and early termination are intended to restrict further reduction, but this selectivity must be measured. Copper sulfide precipitation, thiocyanate formation, polysulfides, and competing homologation can reduce productive flux. The proposed spectrum, dose, and concentrations make the module testable; they do not establish its yield in a natural pool.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by anoxic HCl-bearing volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Shaded, shallow geothermal fracture; aminoacetonitrile 0.1-10 mM; free strong-acid concentration 0.1-1 M after neutralization of incoming ammonia and carbonate. Screen 6-72 hours with aminonitrile, amide, and glycine measurements. A candidate adjacent transport geometry is 1-10 cm of pore length at 1e-5-1e-4 m/s fluid velocity, corresponding to approximately 2 minutes to 3 hours transit; this geometry must be verified, not presumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Protonation of the nitrile nitrogen facilitates water addition and tautomerization to the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of Strecker aminonitriles is established chemistry (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Acidity and temperature are specified explicitly because a generic hydrothermal label does not establish hydrolysis. HCl-bearing volcanic condensate can provide acid without molecular oxygen, but sustained free acidity requires low rock-buffering capacity and sufficient acid supply to overcome the incoming alkalinity. Transport is acceptable only if aminoacetonitrile survives the measured transit and mixing interval.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions supplied by anoxic HCl-bearing volcanic water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same acidic receiver as rxn_006; free strong acid 0.1-1 M after mixing; amide typically 0.1-10 mM. Follow conversion over 12-168 hours rather than assuming nitrile disappearance proves glycine formation. Export to a cooler, shaded compartment after product accumulation. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed water attack on the amide carbonyl produces a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage to the glycine acid-base pool and ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Complete conversion of an aminonitrile to an amino acid requires amide hydrolysis as well as nitrile hydration (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). This separately represented step prevents an unsupported assumption that aminoacetonitrile disappearance equals glycine yield. The main operating questions are completion time and glycine survival in the same hot fluid; both require time-resolved measurements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs capture of aminoacetonitrile as hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ammonia/ammonium bicarbonate buffer for nitrogen and inorganic-carbon speciation control\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar total, with a CO2-bearing gas phase\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Covered, shaded pore or concentrated pool; pH 8.3-9.0; aminoacetonitrile 1-10 mM; total dissolved inorganic carbon 0.1-0.5 M; total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M. Maintain inorganic-carbon inventory through bicarbonate-bearing fluid or gas exchange, rather than adding an unspecified trace of CO2. Initial residence-time screen 1-7 days.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amino-group carbamylation followed by intramolecular cyclization and rearrangement of the aminonitrile-derived intermediate forms hydantoin. The displayed equation is the balanced net aminonitrile-plus-CO2 transformation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin formation from the carbonyl/cyanide/ammonium-carbonate manifold is supported by Bucherer-Bergs chemistry (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934; Ware, 1950). Supplying CO2 alone is not sufficient evidence of useful conversion; ammonia activity, inorganic-carbon speciation, and concentration are specified. Hydantoin is included as a possible chemically distinct reservoir, not assumed to improve survival or yield without measurements in the actual feed mixture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions in strongly acidic volcanic hydrothermal water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423-453 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hydantoin 0.1-10 mM; free strong acid 0.5-2 M after neutralizing the ammonium-carbonate feed; proposed screening interval 24-168 hours. Requires a silica-rich, low-buffering fracture supplied by concentrated anoxic volcanic acid. This is not assigned to an alkaline vent. Track hydantoin, hydantoic acid, glycine, and decomposition products independently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed attack of water at the hydantoin carbonyl opens the cyclic ureide to N-carbamoylglycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry, but often requires forcing conditions (Ware, 1950). This is a major bottleneck, not a presumed consequence of heating. The specified acidic window is a proposed test, and its persistent natural availability is uncertain because rocks neutralize acid. Reject the hydantoin branch if ring opening is slower than precursor loss or subsequent glycine destruction; the network does not claim this comparison has already been demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoic acid hydrolysis and decarbamoylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions in acidic volcanic hydrothermal water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"413-433 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"15-40 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Free strong acid 0.5-2 M; hydantoic acid 0.1-10 mM; proposed 12-168 hour monitoring interval in the same receiver or a slightly cooler downstream pore. Maintain liquid water and account for dissolved or gas-phase CO2. Cool the product-bearing outflow below 333 K after glycine formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the carbamoyl substituent releases glycine; the detached carbamic-acid equivalent decomposes to ammonia and carbon dioxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"N-Carbamoyl amino acids are recognized intermediates in hydantoin-to-amino-acid conversion (Ware, 1950). Ring opening alone does not prove complete glycine release, so decarbamoylation is explicit. Its rate and glycine survival must be determined under the same acid and temperature conditions. No storage advantage or integrated yield is assigned to the hydantoin branch without those measurements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"CO-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate on mixed nickel-iron sulfides\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Freshly precipitated nickel sulfide, proposed loading 1-5 g/L\",\n        \"Freshly precipitated iron sulfide, proposed loading 5-20 g/L\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373-393 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 bar total, including 1-5 bar CO\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Mixed NiS/FeS surfaces; the nickel-containing phase is essential to the proposed catalytic function\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, gas-fed geothermal pore; glyoxylate 1-10 mM; total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M; pH of a cooled 298 K aliquot 7.5-8.5, with in situ speciation calculated separately. Proposed residence-time screen 24-168 hours. Limit additional free sulfide to avoid uncontrolled surface conversion and compare against FeS-only, NiS-only, and no-CO controls.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate and ammonia access an imine-like surface intermediate. Oxidation of CO to CO2 supplies the reducing equivalents required for conversion to glycine. The precise surface intermediates are unresolved.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"CO-driven reductive amination associated with nickel/iron sulfides has laboratory precedent (Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 2003). The numerical window and glyoxylate application here remain a conditional operating proposal, not a claim that every listed combination was demonstrated in that study. Unlike an FeS/H2S-to-pyrite argument, this reaction identifies CO as the consumed reductant and a nickel-bearing catalyst as the proposed kinetic agent. Glycolate formation, catalyst restructuring, and ammonia-dependent selectivity still require direct measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate on a nickel-rich alloy\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Freshly exposed awaruite-like Ni-Fe alloy, approximately Ni2-3Fe; proposed loading 5-20 g/L with measured surface area\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373-413 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar total, with 20-80 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Nickel-rich metallic Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Sulfide-poor serpentinization-related pore; glyoxylate 1-10 mM; total ammonia 0.2-1 M; cooled-aliquot pH 8.5-9.5; dissolved sulfide below 10 micromolar as an initial screening limit. Test 6-168 hours. Quantify actual metallic surface retention, glycine, glycolate, and residual glyoxylate; alloy mass alone is not a measure of catalytic activity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface dissociation of H2 supplies adsorbed hydrogen for reduction of a glyoxylate-derived imine or iminium intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a natural-mineral operating module: the chemical logic of catalytic reductive amination does not establish selective activity of natural awaruite in water. No supplied study validates this exact catalyst-substrate-fluid combination. The alloy is chosen for a specific proposed function, H2 activation, and H2 is explicitly the reductant. High ammonia activity addresses imine formation, while the sulfide restriction addresses catalyst poisoning. Direct carbonyl reduction, passivation, and insufficient active area remain explicit failure modes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of glyoxylic acid oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded pool; pH 5.5-6.5; glyoxylate 1-10 mM; hydroxylamine 1.0-1.5 molar equivalents relative to glyoxylate; initial monitoring over 1-48 hours. Avoid simultaneous contact with reactive metal surfaces before oxime formation. The node does not distinguish E/Z oxime configurations.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine adds to the glyoxylate carbonyl pool, followed by dehydration to the oxime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxime formation is established carbonyl chemistry, and hydroxylamine-based access to amino-acid chemistry has been used in model prebiotic networks (Muchowska et al., 2019). That precedent does not establish a natural concentrated hydroxylamine supply. This branch is therefore conditional at its feedstock boundary, even though the condensation itself is chemically well founded. Hydroxylamine is not silently substituted for ammonia or assumed to be produced by the other pathways.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation and N-O bond cleavage of glyoxylic acid oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Freshly exposed awaruite-like Ni-Fe alloy, approximately Ni2-3Fe; proposed loading 5-20 g/L with measured surface area\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-393 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar total, with 20-80 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Nickel-rich metallic Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Oxime 0.1-10 mM; cooled-aliquot pH 7.5-8.5 after mild neutralization of the formation solution; dissolved sulfide below 10 micromolar as an initial screening limit. Proposed 6-168 hour test interval. Deliver the oxime through a shaded adjacent pore and determine its hydrolysis half-life in the receiving fluid. Monitor glycine, glyoxylate, glycolate, and nitrogen-containing side products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Catalytic H2 activation supports reduction of the C=N bond and cleavage of the N-O bond, giving glycine and water. The net transformation requires four reducing equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative natural-alloy extension: preparative oxime hydrogenation does not prove selective glycine production on awaruite under these conditions, and no supplied study establishes this exact module. H2 pressure, catalyst identity, surface loading, and poisoning limits are specified so the proposal is falsifiable. This branch avoids assuming that sacrificial Fe(0) automatically reduces an oxime selectively. Oxime hydrolysis, competing hydrogenation products, and alloy passivation remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN oligomerization through a transient aminomalononitrile pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Cyanide ion from HCN dissociation, acting within the base-promoted oligomerization manifold\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-313 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar in a covered, poorly ventilated pore\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Intrinsic cyanide-mediated oligomerization; no mineral catalyst assigned\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.5-9.3; total cyanide 0.1-0.5 M; anoxic and UV-shaded. Use time-resolved sampling from 1 hour to 14 days rather than assuming the trimer accumulates. Such concentrations require exceptional local concentration and HCN retention. Competing tetramer and higher-polymer formation must be quantified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Successive cyanide additions, proton transfers, and imine rearrangements within HCN oligomerization can access the aminomalononitrile trimer. This node is a proposed transient component of a mixture, not the sole product of HCN polymerization.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amino-acid production after hydrolysis of concentrated HCN reaction mixtures is experimentally established, and aminomalononitrile belongs to the proposed low-oligomer chemistry (Or\u00f3 and Kamat, 1961; Ferris and Hagan, 1984). Those observations do not establish this resolved trimer sequence as the dominant glycine source. This is explicitly a lower-confidence mechanistic branch. Its critical test is detecting sufficient aminomalononitrile flux before further oligomerization, not merely observing an HCN-derived precipitate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of aminomalononitrile to aminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ions from anoxic volcanic acid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Free strong acid 0.1-1 M after neutralizing the cyanide-rich feed; transient trimer concentration must be measured rather than assigned from total polymer mass. Proposed monitoring interval 6-168 hours. A centimeter-scale acidic receiving pore is required if trimer lifetime precludes longer transport. Aminomalonic acid may decarboxylate as it forms and need not accumulate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential hydration and hydrolysis of both nitrile groups, through amide-containing intermediates, produces aminomalonic acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydrolysis provides a chemically consistent connection between an HCN trimer and a decarboxylating amino dicarboxylic acid. HCN-mixture hydrolysis is supported in aggregate, but this particular resolved intermediate sequence remains mechanistically conditional (Or\u00f3 and Kamat, 1961; Ferris and Hagan, 1984). Selective trimer recovery, competing hydrolysis products, and transport survival are not established by the net equation. This step is not treated as proof that all HCN polymer carbon becomes glycine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Decarboxylation of aminomalonic acid to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-30 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same acidic receiver as rxn_016; free strong acid 0.1-1 M. Follow formation over 1-48 hours after the amino dicarboxylate becomes detectable, with longer observation if required. Account for CO2 partitioning into any gas phase and cool the glycine-bearing outflow after conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermally promoted decarboxylation of the amino-substituted malonic acid skeleton releases CO2; proton transfer gives glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Decarboxylation is chemically consistent with the malonic-acid skeleton and has been invoked in discussions of amino-acid formation from HCN oligomers (Ferris and Hagan, 1984). The principal uncertainty for this pathway is upstream aminomalononitrile formation and survival, not atom conservation in the decarboxylation. Nevertheless, the specified residence interval is a test window rather than a measured rate, and glycine selectivity must be established in the actual hydrolysate.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-imine Strecker route with direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia generate a transient imine in the presence of cyanide. Aminoacetonitrile is then hydrolyzed through glycinamide in an adjacent acidic heated-water compartment. This has the strongest established chemical core; productive operation still requires verified acid supply and precursor survival.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-cyanohydrin Strecker route with direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde is first trapped as glycolonitrile. Ammonia enrichment moves the carbonyl-cyanide equilibrium into aminoacetonitrile, followed by hydration and amide hydrolysis. This is a reservoir-entry alternative to P1, not an additional independent carbon source.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN entry with direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Sulfide-driven Cu(I) photochemistry supplies glycolonitrile from HCN without requiring selective capture of free methanimine. Shading and ammonia enrichment precede aminonitrile formation and acidic hydrolysis. Photon-dependent selectivity and compatibility of the transferred photochemical mixture are explicit bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry Strecker route through a hydantoin reservoir\",\n      \"description\": \"The formaldehyde-imine Strecker sequence supplies aminoacetonitrile, which enters the ammonium-carbonate-dependent hydantoin manifold. Ring opening and decarbamoylation subsequently release glycine. This pathway is conditional on both useful hydantoin formation and sufficiently fast hydrolysis under available acidity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-entry Strecker route through hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolonitrile releases its carbon into the ammonia-rich Strecker manifold before hydantoin formation. Glycine production requires demonstrated release from both reservoirs; neither glycolonitrile nor hydantoin accumulation alone establishes productive flux.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical HCN entry through the hydantoin branch\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN photoreductive homologation supplies glycolonitrile, followed by ammonia-dependent aminonitrile formation and hydantoin capture. Acidic heated water releases glycine through hydantoic acid. This combines two conditional modules and is not claimed to be an experimentally integrated sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"CO-driven nickel-iron-sulfide reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylate and ammonia undergo proposed CO-driven reductive amination on nickel-containing sulfides. CO oxidation, rather than unspecified pyrite formation, supplies reducing equivalents. The reaction class has laboratory precedent, while the stated glyoxylate operating window requires validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven alloy-catalyzed reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied glyoxylate and ammonia are converted directly to glycine using H2 and a proposed awaruite-like catalyst. This is a lower-confidence natural-mineral extrapolation requiring evidence for active-surface persistence and imine-selective reduction rather than glycolate formation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxime formation followed by catalytic hydrogenation\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate is condensed with supplied hydroxylamine to form an oxime, which is transferred to a sulfide-poor, H2-bearing catalytic compartment. The pathway differs chemically from ammonia-based reductive amination but remains conditional on hydroxylamine supply and selective natural-alloy hydrogenation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-trimer hydrolysis and decarboxylation\",\n      \"description\": \"A proposed transient aminomalononitrile fraction from concentrated HCN oligomerization is hydrolyzed to aminomalonic acid, which decarboxylates to glycine. Amino-acid formation from HCN hydrolysates has experimental precedent, but assigning sufficient flux specifically to this resolved trimer sequence remains a lower-confidence mechanistic hypothesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_017\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "An evidence-graded candidate network containing ten distinct reaction sequences: six combinations of Strecker precursor formation with either direct hydrolysis or hydantoin-mediated release, two glyoxylate reductive-amination routes, one glyoxylate oxime route, and one HCN-oligomer route. The strongest chemical support is for the Strecker core. Photochemical selectivity, hydantoin hydrolysis under geochemically available acidity, natural-alloy catalysis, and the resolved HCN-trimer pathway remain conditional rather than demonstrated integrated syntheses. Numerical conditions below are proposed operating or screening windows, not invented experimental yields or residence-time guarantees. Formaldehyde, HCN, glyoxylate, ammonia, hydroxylamine, CO, H2, sulfide, water, and inorganic carbon are declared boundary feedstocks; their simultaneous natural availability is not assumed. Surface and heated-water stages occupy adjacent compartments of a subaerial geothermal system, rather than requiring transport to a distant deep-sea vent. Methanimine is generated and captured in the same solution and is never transported as an isolated reservoir. Stable precursor solutions may drain through shaded, centimeter-scale fractures, but transfer is acceptable only if measured transit time is shorter than precursor loss times. All stages are anoxic. Neutral molecular formulas represent reference acid-base forms; actual protonation, hydration, and inorganic-carbon speciation follow the stated pH. Mineral agents are included only for an identified chemical function. S8 is a terminal redox byproduct labeled intermediate because the required role vocabulary has no byproduct category. The network is complete at its declared feedstock boundary, not a claim that ten complete pathways have been experimentally demonstrated under natural conditions.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CH3N", "iupac_name": "methanimine", "common_name": "Formaldimine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH3N/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "Glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "Aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetamide", "common_name": "Glycinamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H4N2O2", "iupac_name": "imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "Hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H6N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)acetic acid", "common_name": "Hydantoic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "Carbon monoxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C2H3NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid", "common_name": "Glyoxylic acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C3H3N3", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanedinitrile", "common_name": "Aminomalononitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C3H5NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanedioic acid", "common_name": "Aminomalonic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H5NO4/c4-1(2(5)6)3(7)8/h1H,4H2,(H,5,6)(H,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reversible formation of the Strecker imine", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, mineral-shaded pool or covered pore; pH 8.7-9.5 measured at reaction temperature; total formaldehyde 1-10 mM and total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M. Cyanide for rxn_002 is present from the start at 10-100 mM total concentration. Evaluate the coupled reactions over 6-72 hours. Concentrations denote analytical totals, including hydrated formaldehyde and ammonium."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the carbonyl pool through a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to methanimine or its conjugate acid. This node represents a transient intermediate, not an isolatable bulk stock.", "reasoning": "The imine-capture manifold is supported by classical Strecker chemistry and studies of amino-acid formation in prebiotic mixtures (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Formaldehyde hydration, imine hydrolysis, and competing formaldehyde-ammonia condensations prevent an assumption of quantitative imine accumulation. Simultaneous cyanide trapping, rather than storage or transport of methanimine, is the enabling design choice. The stated concentration and time ranges are proposed operating windows."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide capture of methanimine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same solution and residence interval as rxn_001; pH 8.7-9.5; total cyanide 10-100 mM and total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M. Maintain cyanide in excess over total formaldehyde. Exclude direct UV irradiation and avoid an open, strongly ventilated surface that would rapidly lose HCN."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to produce aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the glycine Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). It requires no speculative mineral redox chemistry. Product formation must nevertheless be measured against competing imine hydrolysis and cyanohydrin formation; the latter is included explicitly as an alternative entry rather than treated as an unexplained loss."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Formaldehyde trapping as glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded aqueous compartment; pH 8.0-9.3; total formaldehyde 1-10 mM and total cyanide 10-100 mM. Initial sampling over 10 minutes to 24 hours. Low ammonia favors identification of the cyanohydrin entry; ammonia enrichment occurs in rxn_004."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to formaldehyde and the resulting alkoxide is protonated, yielding the cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is established aqueous carbonyl chemistry and is relevant to both classical prebiotic Strecker mixtures and cyanosulfidic chemistry (Miller, 1957; Patel et al., 2015). Glycolonitrile is a chemically justified formaldehyde reservoir, but reservoir formation alone does not demonstrate subsequent aminoacetonitrile production. Its productive release is therefore represented and assessed separately."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of the cyanohydrin reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-333 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in a covered, gas-exchange-limited pore", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 9.0-9.7; glycolonitrile 0.1-10 mM; total ammonia 0.2-0.5 M; total cyanide preferably 10-100 mM; evaluate for 12-168 hours. For photochemical feed, stop irradiation before ammonia enrichment, settle suspended sulfur, and measure residual sulfide and dissolved copper rather than assuming filtration removes them."}, "mechanism": "Glycolonitrile reversibly releases formaldehyde and cyanide; ammonia accesses the imine manifold, and cyanide recapture yields aminoacetonitrile. This is a net equilibrium-mediated transformation, not an assumed direct displacement of hydroxide.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile conversion connects established carbonyl/cyanide/ammonia equilibria (Miller, 1957; Patel et al., 2015). Elevated ammonia activity is essential. The proposed ammonia concentration requires a locally concentrated ammonium-bearing pool, not ordinary seawater. Productive conversion in sulfide- and copper-containing photochemical effluent remains an integration test, so no quantitative flux is assumed from glycolonitrile accumulation alone."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation of HCN to glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)-cyanide photocatalyst: CuCN-derived soluble cyanocuprate complexes, 10-100 micromolar total copper"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-313 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cu(I)-cyanide complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic illuminated film 0.1-1 mm thick; pH 7.5-8.5; total cyanide 20-100 mM; total sulfide supplied in limiting pulses of 0.5-2 mM. Use a 254 nm laboratory reference band at 0.05-0.5 mW/cm2 incident irradiance for an initial 1-12 hour screening interval, recording the absorbed photon dose. Natural operation requires an equivalent usable dose from the actual 200-280 nm surface spectrum; photons below 200 nm are not assumed. Dissolved Fe2+ should remain below approximately 50 micromolar through local FeS buffering, despite abundant reduced iron in surrounding rock."}, "mechanism": "Cu-mediated photoredox chemistry transfers reducing equivalents from sulfide to HCN. Hydrolysis of the reduced C1 nitrogen intermediate generates formaldehyde equivalents, which excess cyanide traps as glycolonitrile. Sulfide oxidation is represented by elemental sulfur; the equation is a lumped productive branch, not a claim that the complete sulfur product distribution is exclusively S8.", "reasoning": "Cyanosulfidic photochemistry provides experimental precedent for reductive HCN homologation and cyanohydrin-containing product networks (Patel et al., 2015). This design does not require selective interception of free photogenerated methanimine or unsupported methanol photodehydrogenation. Sulfide limitation and early termination are intended to restrict further reduction, but this selectivity must be measured. Copper sulfide precipitation, thiocyanate formation, polysulfides, and competing homologation can reduce productive flux. The proposed spectrum, dose, and concentrations make the module testable; they do not establish its yield in a natural pool."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acid-catalyzed aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by anoxic HCl-bearing volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "10-30 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Shaded, shallow geothermal fracture; aminoacetonitrile 0.1-10 mM; free strong-acid concentration 0.1-1 M after neutralization of incoming ammonia and carbonate. Screen 6-72 hours with aminonitrile, amide, and glycine measurements. A candidate adjacent transport geometry is 1-10 cm of pore length at 1e-5-1e-4 m/s fluid velocity, corresponding to approximately 2 minutes to 3 hours transit; this geometry must be verified, not presumed."}, "mechanism": "Protonation of the nitrile nitrogen facilitates water addition and tautomerization to the amide.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis of Strecker aminonitriles is established chemistry (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Acidity and temperature are specified explicitly because a generic hydrothermal label does not establish hydrolysis. HCl-bearing volcanic condensate can provide acid without molecular oxygen, but sustained free acidity requires low rock-buffering capacity and sufficient acid supply to overcome the incoming alkalinity. Transport is acceptable only if aminoacetonitrile survives the measured transit and mixing interval."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions supplied by anoxic HCl-bearing volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "10-30 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same acidic receiver as rxn_006; free strong acid 0.1-1 M after mixing; amide typically 0.1-10 mM. Follow conversion over 12-168 hours rather than assuming nitrile disappearance proves glycine formation. Export to a cooler, shaded compartment after product accumulation. Released ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed water attack on the amide carbonyl produces a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage to the glycine acid-base pool and ammonium.", "reasoning": "Complete conversion of an aminonitrile to an amino acid requires amide hydrolysis as well as nitrile hydration (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). This separately represented step prevents an unsupported assumption that aminoacetonitrile disappearance equals glycine yield. The main operating questions are completion time and glycine survival in the same hot fluid; both require time-resolved measurements."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs capture of aminoacetonitrile as hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ammonia/ammonium bicarbonate buffer for nitrogen and inorganic-carbon speciation control"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar total, with a CO2-bearing gas phase", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Covered, shaded pore or concentrated pool; pH 8.3-9.0; aminoacetonitrile 1-10 mM; total dissolved inorganic carbon 0.1-0.5 M; total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M. Maintain inorganic-carbon inventory through bicarbonate-bearing fluid or gas exchange, rather than adding an unspecified trace of CO2. Initial residence-time screen 1-7 days."}, "mechanism": "Amino-group carbamylation followed by intramolecular cyclization and rearrangement of the aminonitrile-derived intermediate forms hydantoin. The displayed equation is the balanced net aminonitrile-plus-CO2 transformation.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin formation from the carbonyl/cyanide/ammonium-carbonate manifold is supported by Bucherer-Bergs chemistry (Bucherer and Lieb, 1934; Ware, 1950). Supplying CO2 alone is not sufficient evidence of useful conversion; ammonia activity, inorganic-carbon speciation, and concentration are specified. Hydantoin is included as a possible chemically distinct reservoir, not assumed to improve survival or yield without measurements in the actual feed mixture."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydantoin ring opening to hydantoic acid", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions in strongly acidic volcanic hydrothermal water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "423-453 K", "pressure": "20-50 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Hydantoin 0.1-10 mM; free strong acid 0.5-2 M after neutralizing the ammonium-carbonate feed; proposed screening interval 24-168 hours. Requires a silica-rich, low-buffering fracture supplied by concentrated anoxic volcanic acid. This is not assigned to an alkaline vent. Track hydantoin, hydantoic acid, glycine, and decomposition products independently."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed attack of water at the hydantoin carbonyl opens the cyclic ureide to N-carbamoylglycine.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis is established preparative chemistry, but often requires forcing conditions (Ware, 1950). This is a major bottleneck, not a presumed consequence of heating. The specified acidic window is a proposed test, and its persistent natural availability is uncertain because rocks neutralize acid. Reject the hydantoin branch if ring opening is slower than precursor loss or subsequent glycine destruction; the network does not claim this comparison has already been demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydantoic acid hydrolysis and decarbamoylation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003", "mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions in acidic volcanic hydrothermal water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "413-433 K", "pressure": "15-40 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Free strong acid 0.5-2 M; hydantoic acid 0.1-10 mM; proposed 12-168 hour monitoring interval in the same receiver or a slightly cooler downstream pore. Maintain liquid water and account for dissolved or gas-phase CO2. Cool the product-bearing outflow below 333 K after glycine formation."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the carbamoyl substituent releases glycine; the detached carbamic-acid equivalent decomposes to ammonia and carbon dioxide.", "reasoning": "N-Carbamoyl amino acids are recognized intermediates in hydantoin-to-amino-acid conversion (Ware, 1950). Ring opening alone does not prove complete glycine release, so decarbamoylation is explicit. Its rate and glycine survival must be determined under the same acid and temperature conditions. No storage advantage or integrated yield is assigned to the hydantoin branch without those measurements."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "CO-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate on mixed nickel-iron sulfides", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Freshly precipitated nickel sulfide, proposed loading 1-5 g/L", "Freshly precipitated iron sulfide, proposed loading 5-20 g/L"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373-393 K", "pressure": "5-20 bar total, including 1-5 bar CO", "catalyst": "Mixed NiS/FeS surfaces; the nickel-containing phase is essential to the proposed catalytic function", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, gas-fed geothermal pore; glyoxylate 1-10 mM; total ammonia 0.1-0.5 M; pH of a cooled 298 K aliquot 7.5-8.5, with in situ speciation calculated separately. Proposed residence-time screen 24-168 hours. Limit additional free sulfide to avoid uncontrolled surface conversion and compare against FeS-only, NiS-only, and no-CO controls."}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylate and ammonia access an imine-like surface intermediate. Oxidation of CO to CO2 supplies the reducing equivalents required for conversion to glycine. The precise surface intermediates are unresolved.", "reasoning": "CO-driven reductive amination associated with nickel/iron sulfides has laboratory precedent (Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 2003). The numerical window and glyoxylate application here remain a conditional operating proposal, not a claim that every listed combination was demonstrated in that study. Unlike an FeS/H2S-to-pyrite argument, this reaction identifies CO as the consumed reductant and a nickel-bearing catalyst as the proposed kinetic agent. Glycolate formation, catalyst restructuring, and ammonia-dependent selectivity still require direct measurement."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of glyoxylate on a nickel-rich alloy", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Freshly exposed awaruite-like Ni-Fe alloy, approximately Ni2-3Fe; proposed loading 5-20 g/L with measured surface area"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373-413 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar total, with 20-80 bar H2", "catalyst": "Nickel-rich metallic Ni-Fe alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-poor serpentinization-related pore; glyoxylate 1-10 mM; total ammonia 0.2-1 M; cooled-aliquot pH 8.5-9.5; dissolved sulfide below 10 micromolar as an initial screening limit. Test 6-168 hours. Quantify actual metallic surface retention, glycine, glycolate, and residual glyoxylate; alloy mass alone is not a measure of catalytic activity."}, "mechanism": "Surface dissociation of H2 supplies adsorbed hydrogen for reduction of a glyoxylate-derived imine or iminium intermediate.", "reasoning": "Speculative as a natural-mineral operating module: the chemical logic of catalytic reductive amination does not establish selective activity of natural awaruite in water. No supplied study validates this exact catalyst-substrate-fluid combination. The alloy is chosen for a specific proposed function, H2 activation, and H2 is explicitly the reductant. High ammonia activity addresses imine formation, while the sulfide restriction addresses catalyst poisoning. Direct carbonyl reduction, passivation, and insufficient active area remain explicit failure modes."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Formation of glyoxylic acid oxime", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded pool; pH 5.5-6.5; glyoxylate 1-10 mM; hydroxylamine 1.0-1.5 molar equivalents relative to glyoxylate; initial monitoring over 1-48 hours. Avoid simultaneous contact with reactive metal surfaces before oxime formation. The node does not distinguish E/Z oxime configurations."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine adds to the glyoxylate carbonyl pool, followed by dehydration to the oxime.", "reasoning": "Oxime formation is established carbonyl chemistry, and hydroxylamine-based access to amino-acid chemistry has been used in model prebiotic networks (Muchowska et al., 2019). That precedent does not establish a natural concentrated hydroxylamine supply. This branch is therefore conditional at its feedstock boundary, even though the condensation itself is chemically well founded. Hydroxylamine is not silently substituted for ammonia or assumed to be produced by the other pathways."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Hydrogenation and N-O bond cleavage of glyoxylic acid oxime", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Freshly exposed awaruite-like Ni-Fe alloy, approximately Ni2-3Fe; proposed loading 5-20 g/L with measured surface area"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-393 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar total, with 20-80 bar H2", "catalyst": "Nickel-rich metallic Ni-Fe alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Oxime 0.1-10 mM; cooled-aliquot pH 7.5-8.5 after mild neutralization of the formation solution; dissolved sulfide below 10 micromolar as an initial screening limit. Proposed 6-168 hour test interval. Deliver the oxime through a shaded adjacent pore and determine its hydrolysis half-life in the receiving fluid. Monitor glycine, glyoxylate, glycolate, and nitrogen-containing side products."}, "mechanism": "Catalytic H2 activation supports reduction of the C=N bond and cleavage of the N-O bond, giving glycine and water. The net transformation requires four reducing equivalents.", "reasoning": "Speculative natural-alloy extension: preparative oxime hydrogenation does not prove selective glycine production on awaruite under these conditions, and no supplied study establishes this exact module. H2 pressure, catalyst identity, surface loading, and poisoning limits are specified so the proposal is falsifiable. This branch avoids assuming that sacrificial Fe(0) automatically reduces an oxime selectively. Oxime hydrolysis, competing hydrogenation products, and alloy passivation remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "HCN oligomerization through a transient aminomalononitrile pool", "inputs": ["mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cyanide ion from HCN dissociation, acting within the base-promoted oligomerization manifold"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-313 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar in a covered, poorly ventilated pore", "catalyst": "Intrinsic cyanide-mediated oligomerization; no mineral catalyst assigned", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.5-9.3; total cyanide 0.1-0.5 M; anoxic and UV-shaded. Use time-resolved sampling from 1 hour to 14 days rather than assuming the trimer accumulates. Such concentrations require exceptional local concentration and HCN retention. Competing tetramer and higher-polymer formation must be quantified."}, "mechanism": "Successive cyanide additions, proton transfers, and imine rearrangements within HCN oligomerization can access the aminomalononitrile trimer. This node is a proposed transient component of a mixture, not the sole product of HCN polymerization.", "reasoning": "Amino-acid production after hydrolysis of concentrated HCN reaction mixtures is experimentally established, and aminomalononitrile belongs to the proposed low-oligomer chemistry (Or\u00f3 and Kamat, 1961; Ferris and Hagan, 1984). Those observations do not establish this resolved trimer sequence as the dominant glycine source. This is explicitly a lower-confidence mechanistic branch. Its critical test is detecting sufficient aminomalononitrile flux before further oligomerization, not merely observing an HCN-derived precipitate."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Hydrolysis of aminomalononitrile to aminomalonic acid", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from anoxic volcanic acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "10-30 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Free strong acid 0.1-1 M after neutralizing the cyanide-rich feed; transient trimer concentration must be measured rather than assigned from total polymer mass. Proposed monitoring interval 6-168 hours. A centimeter-scale acidic receiving pore is required if trimer lifetime precludes longer transport. Aminomalonic acid may decarboxylate as it forms and need not accumulate."}, "mechanism": "Sequential hydration and hydrolysis of both nitrile groups, through amide-containing intermediates, produces aminomalonic acid and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Nitrile hydrolysis provides a chemically consistent connection between an HCN trimer and a decarboxylating amino dicarboxylic acid. HCN-mixture hydrolysis is supported in aggregate, but this particular resolved intermediate sequence remains mechanistically conditional (Or\u00f3 and Kamat, 1961; Ferris and Hagan, 1984). Selective trimer recovery, competing hydrolysis products, and transport survival are not established by the net equation. This step is not treated as proof that all HCN polymer carbon becomes glycine."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Decarboxylation of aminomalonic acid to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "10-30 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same acidic receiver as rxn_016; free strong acid 0.1-1 M. Follow formation over 1-48 hours after the amino dicarboxylate becomes detectable, with longer observation if required. Account for CO2 partitioning into any gas phase and cool the glycine-bearing outflow after conversion."}, "mechanism": "Thermally promoted decarboxylation of the amino-substituted malonic acid skeleton releases CO2; proton transfer gives glycine.", "reasoning": "Decarboxylation is chemically consistent with the malonic-acid skeleton and has been invoked in discussions of amino-acid formation from HCN oligomers (Ferris and Hagan, 1984). The principal uncertainty for this pathway is upstream aminomalononitrile formation and survival, not atom conservation in the decarboxylation. Nevertheless, the specified residence interval is a test window rather than a measured rate, and glycine selectivity must be established in the actual hydrolysate."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Formaldehyde-imine Strecker route with direct hydrolysis", "description": "Formaldehyde and ammonia generate a transient imine in the presence of cyanide. Aminoacetonitrile is then hydrolyzed through glycinamide in an adjacent acidic heated-water compartment. This has the strongest established chemical core; productive operation still requires verified acid supply and precursor survival.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Formaldehyde-cyanohydrin Strecker route with direct hydrolysis", "description": "Formaldehyde is first trapped as glycolonitrile. Ammonia enrichment moves the carbonyl-cyanide equilibrium into aminoacetonitrile, followed by hydration and amide hydrolysis. This is a reservoir-entry alternative to P1, not an additional independent carbon source.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Photochemical HCN entry with direct Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Sulfide-driven Cu(I) photochemistry supplies glycolonitrile from HCN without requiring selective capture of free methanimine. Shading and ammonia enrichment precede aminonitrile formation and acidic hydrolysis. Photon-dependent selectivity and compatibility of the transferred photochemical mixture are explicit bottlenecks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Imine-entry Strecker route through a hydantoin reservoir", "description": "The formaldehyde-imine Strecker sequence supplies aminoacetonitrile, which enters the ammonium-carbonate-dependent hydantoin manifold. Ring opening and decarbamoylation subsequently release glycine. This pathway is conditional on both useful hydantoin formation and sufficiently fast hydrolysis under available acidity.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Cyanohydrin-entry Strecker route through hydantoin", "description": "Glycolonitrile releases its carbon into the ammonia-rich Strecker manifold before hydantoin formation. Glycine production requires demonstrated release from both reservoirs; neither glycolonitrile nor hydantoin accumulation alone establishes productive flux.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Photochemical HCN entry through the hydantoin branch", 