m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glycine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: An evidence-graded candidate network containing ten distinct reaction sequences: six combinations of Strecker precursor formation with either direct hydrolysis or hydantoin-mediated release, two glyoxylate reductive-amination routes, one glyoxylate oxime route, and one HCN-oligomer route. The strongest chemical support is for the Strecker core. Photochemical selectivity, hydantoin hydrolysis under geochemically available acidity, natural-alloy catalysis, and the resolved HCN-trimer pathway remain conditional rather than demonstrated integrated syntheses. Numerical conditions below are proposed operating or screening windows, not invented experimental yields or residence-time guarantees. Formaldehyde, HCN, glyoxylate, ammonia, hydroxylamine, CO, H2, sulfide, water, and inorganic carbon are declared boundary feedstocks; their simultaneous natural availability is not assumed. Surface and heated-water stages occupy adjacent compartments of a subaerial geothermal system, rather than requiring transport to a distant deep-sea vent. Methanimine is generated and captured in the same solution and is never transported as an isolated reservoir. Stable precursor solutions may drain through shaded, centimeter-scale fractures, but 